{"code":"AFRST 146W","title":"Black Women's Lit Renaissance","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"The twenty-year period between 1970 and 1990 is often referred to as the \"Black Women's Literary Renaissance\" due to the unprecedented amount of literary and cultural production from black women in the United States. This course explores its profound contributions to black life and literature. Students will examine the intersections and oppositions among political movements, such as the Lesbian Feminist Movement and the Black Power Movement, and identify how these connections shaped the aesthetic landscape of the time. Course materials will include essays, novels, poems, and visual art, featuring Alice Walker, Toni Cade Bambara, bell hooks, Lorna Simpson, and others.","cross_listed":["LIT 146W","WMGST 146W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-146","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only.","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 159","title":"Elementary Jazz Dance","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An introductory technique course exploring jazz dance as a form grounded in Black American cultural and performance traditions. Students develop foundational skills in rhythm, coordination, and jazz vocabulary while situating studio practice within the historical evolution of jazz dance. Attention is given to stylistic nuance, musicality, and expressive range within jazz movement. Open to all students; no previous dance experience required.","cross_listed":["DANCE 159"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/100/afrst-159","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Blood Fitness Center","capacity":25,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"101","type":"LEC","building":"Blood Fitness Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Nikolas C. Owens"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 201W","title":"Diversity Reconsidered","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An examination of the emergence of Africana Studies as a transdisciplinary field of social and cultural critique and politics. Explores the work of foundational (e.g., W.E.B Dubois and Anna Julia Cooper) and contemporary figures. Topics include but are not limited to the history of Africana thought, race and global racial formation, resistance and politics, intersectionality, gender and sexuality, representation, white privilege, belonging, Blackness, and diaspora.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Donald M. Carter"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 224W","title":"Gender,space,identity","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course examines how racialized and gendered identities are made in relation to space. How does gender intersect with race, class and other power relations embedded in the places we live? How do women and men come to occupy different places in the world literally and figuratively or occupy the same places in different ways? Case studies focus on identity making in relation to the body and the course more broadly focuses on the intersection of race, class, gender, sexuality, and ethnicity as matrices of social and structural power relations and hierarchies.","cross_listed":["WMGST 224W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-224","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Sr, Jrs, Soph, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Merrill"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 232","title":"Toni Morrison","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course is an in-depth study of Toni Morrison's fictional works and critical writing. Students will explore her creative and scholarly contributions to ideas of community and genealogy, as well as to representations of race, gender, and nation. Students will also explore her attention to language, form, and literary theory. In studying both Morrison's formal and theoretical commitments, students will develop an awareness of how literature serves as a vehicle for animating and critiquing social, historical, and ethical concerns in our world. Assignments will include short critical essays, collaborative presentations and projects, and readings will include \"The Source of Self-Regard,\" \"The Bluest Eye,\" \"Song of Solomon,\" and \"Jazz.\" (Genre) (Theory)","cross_listed":["LIT 232"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-232","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 242W","title":"The Black Self","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"A philosophical exploration of a variety of historical and contemporary works that illuminate and influence the phenomenological experience of being Black.","cross_listed":["PHIL 242W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/200/afrst-242","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Todd Franklin"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 267","title":"Jazz Connections","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An advanced jazz course that builds on the topics covered in Jazz Foundations (MUSIC 161), and examines the construction of the \"jazz tradition\" from a historiographical perspective. Considers how the development of jazz, and its construction in the context of educational institutions, both reflects and shapes concepts of race and gender in American culture. Includes an examination of the impact of jazz genres including hip hop, R&B, and gospel, and focuses on the international proliferation of jazz. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AFRST 367","MUSIC 267","MUSIC 367"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 306","title":"Black Europe","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Generations of African diasporic people contest their representation and belonging in contemporary Europe, while the politics surrounding social diversity, economic and social transformations are underway. Through social and political activism, scholarship, literature, film and the visual arts, Black Europeans reclaim colonial and other histories, and generate decolonial social portrayals and meanings. The rise of anti-immigrant and authoritarian politics and militarized borders are part of this changing social historical context. We examine social, political, and artistic dimensions of the environment and how it is perceived, lived and reimagined among Black Europeans in various countries, especially but not exclusively Italy, France, Germany and England.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/300/afrst-306","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Merrill"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 335W","title":"Urban Worlds","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Since the great northern migrations of the World War II era much of African American experience has been lived out in urban worlds, from New York to San Francisco. The city has held out a peculiar hope for a better life, celebrated in fiction, scholarly literature and popular culture, since the Black exodus from the South. We explore the American story of promise and inclusion and the visible and at times hidden workings of exclusion, two stories that unfold simultaneously in the experience of urban America. Prerequisites, One course in Africana Studies or consent of Instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/300/afrst-335","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Donald M. Carter"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 367","title":"Jazz Connections","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"An advanced jazz course that builds on the topics covered in Jazz Foundations (MUSIC 161), and examines the construction of the \"jazz tradition\" from a historiographical perspective. Considers how the development of jazz, and its construction in the context of educational institutions, both reflects and shapes concepts of race and gender in American culture. Includes an examination of the impact of jazz genres including hip hop, R&B, and gospel, and focuses on the international proliferation of jazz. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AFRST 267","MUSIC 267","MUSIC 367"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 405","title":"Sem Black Feminist Thought","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"Interdisciplinary examination of the tradition of black feminist thought as it spans African and African-American heritages.Exploration of how black women are not simply victims of oppression but visionary agents of change.Areas examined include history, literature,music,art,education, sociology and film. There is intersectional analysis of the social, structural, and institutional hierarchies within the identities of women of African descent (gender/transgender, race, white supremacy, sexual/affectional orientation, class, color, and ability). Prerequisites, one course in women's studies or consent of instructor.","cross_listed":["WMGST 405"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/400/afrst-405","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Jessina Emmert"]}]},
{"code":"AFRST 549","title":"Sr Sem Africana Studies","department":"Africana Studies","credits":1,"description":"The research process at it relates to fulfillment of the senior project, including the formulation of a research project, methodological approaches, frames of research, research design, collection of data, and analysis. Culminates in presentation of a thesis/project proposal.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/afrst-africana-studies/500/afrst-549","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
