{"code":"AIIS 243","title":"Indigenous Oral Traditions","department":"American Indian & Indigen Std","credits":1,"description":"This course examines various elements of Indigenous oral traditions, including oral literatures like creation stories, narratives, cautionary tales, formal oratory, dance, and song, theorizing how some of these oral traditions can also be considered forms of writing. We will study different Indigenous methods of performance, analyzing elements such as tone and pitch, gestures, silence, back-channeling, turn-taking, taking of floor, and traditional openings and closings. We will also compare conversational norms, metaphors, puns, and humor used in diverse Indigenous cultures. Finally, we will examine the goals of decolonization in many Indigenous communities and explore the practices and discourses intended to decolonize and/or Indigenize daily life and promote Indigenous identities in the coming generations.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/aiis-american-indian-and-indigenous-studies/200/aiis-243","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Meredith G. Moss"]}]},
{"code":"AIIS 262","title":"Siberian Text","department":"American Indian & Indigen Std","credits":1,"description":"This course is an exploration of five centuries of writing in and about Siberia. While we will root ourselves in the socio-historical context, our main goal is to engage meaningfully with the very process of the formation of the Siberian Text. By this, scholars of the region mean the body of works that emerged out of the geographical, political, anthropological, and cultural categorizations of Siberia. The plurality of voices and the diversity of spatial experiences engaged in the process of creating the Siberian Text include travelers, criminals, political prisoners, religious dissenters, shamans, hunters, ethnographers, missionaries, vagabonds, and peasants. Probing the fraught legacy of the current spatial discourse on Siberia, the course will examine a variety of media and genres, including official documents, travelogues, oral tradition(s), works of fiction, autobiographies, pictorial art, and film. In addition to the transnational and transhistorical dimensions, we will engage with theories of space and place, imagined geographies, and ecocriticism.","cross_listed":["LIT 262","RSNST 262"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/aiis-american-indian-and-indigenous-studies/200/aiis-262","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ani Abrahamyan"]}]},
{"code":"AIIS 310","title":"Emergent Indigeneity","department":"American Indian & Indigen Std","credits":1,"description":"This class will explore Emergent Indigeneity: how Indigenous identity is a dynamic and continually evolving process of redefining and making that rejects fixed notions of \"purity\" or \"authenticity.\" We will look at the ways modern American Indian and First Nations artists draw upon traditional practices, materials, and cosmologies within their tribally-specific knowledge systems, but use modern technologies, methods, and materials to communicate what it means to be Indigenous in the late 20th and early 21st century. For example, we might look at how Native writers redefine a literary genre drawing on specific Indigenous knowledge systems, how some Indigenous pop-music artists sample traditional songs, or how TV writers represent cultural practices in a modern context. Will include literature, film, TV, music, social media, and material culture, as well as beadwork, performance art, and modern art.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/aiis-american-indian-and-indigenous-studies/300/aiis-310","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Brianna R. Burke"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 105","title":"Intro to Native Amer Studies","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"The course serves as an introduction to the interdisciplinary field of Native American Studies, exploring both the complexity and diversity of Native American experiences. Students learn about both historical and contemporary events and issues from Indigenous perspectives and develop new ways of thinking and talking about - and with - Native Americans.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/100/amst-105","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 117","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as Coal Miner's Daughter, Nashville, and O Brother, Where Art Thou? No previous knowledge of music required. AMST-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 217","MUSIC 117","MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 217","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as Coal Miner's Daughter, Nashville, and O Brother, Where Art Thou? No previous knowledge of music required. AMST-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, and 1st Yrs. Open to Music majors/minors only or instructor permission.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 239","title":"Native Amer Religious Freedom","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Is American religious freedom a reality, an unfinished project, or merely a myth? This course explores how Native Americans have struggled for religious freedom in the United States, focusing on contemporary legal battles to protect sacred lands, repatriate ancestral remains and objects, and defend the ceremonial consumption of peyote.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/200/amst-239","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Diversity Course","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 245","title":"Music in Amer Film","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examination of music in American film from silent films to the present with emphasis on the golden age of Hollywood. Topics include the development of musical conventions in film, different approaches of film composers (Steiner, Tiomkin, Rzsa, Herrmann, Newman, Bernstein, Williams), the meanings that music brings to film narratives. Includes films such as \"Casablanca\", \"The Adventures of Robin Hood,\" \"To Kill a Mockingbird,\" \"Of Mice and Men,\" \"A Streetcar Named Desire,\" \"Amadeus,\" \"The Shawshank Redemption,\" \"Oh Brother, Where Art Thou?\" Special attention to Hitchcock films (Psycho, Vertigo, Rear Window). Prerequisites, two courses, in any combination, in music, film, or literature. Three hours per week for film viewings in addition to class time. Music 345 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 345","MUSIC 245","MUSIC 345"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, & 1st Years","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 310","title":"Sem: Indigenous Ecologies","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"This interdisciplinary seminar explores the traditional ecological knowledge (TEK) of indigenous peoples. Drawing upon scholarship from such diverse fields as acoustic ecology, ethno-ecology, ethnography, geography, environmental history, Native American and Indigenous Studies, and religious studies, we will examine indigenous knowledge about particular species and relationships between them.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/300/amst-310","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 345","title":"Music in Amer Film","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examination of music in American film from silent films to the present with emphasis on the golden age of Hollywood. Topics include the development of musical conventions in film, different approaches of film composers (Steiner, Tiomkin, Rzsa, Herrmann, Newman, Bernstein, Williams), the meanings that music brings to film narratives. Includes films such as Casablanca, The Adventures of Robin Hood, To Kill a Mockingbird, Of Mice and Men, A Streetcar Named Desire, Amadeus, The Shawshank Redemption, Oh Brother, Where Art Thou? Special attention to Hitchcock films (Psycho, Vertigo, Rear Window). Three hours per week for film viewings in addition to class time. Music 345 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["MUSIC 245","MUSIC 345"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Sophomores, & 1st Yrs. Open to Music majors/minors only or instructor permission required.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 500","title":"Senior Project","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"This is a capstone course limited to senior concentrators in American Studies. Students conduct a project on a topic in American Studies.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/500/amst-500","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"AMST 550","title":"Honors Thesis","department":"American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Independent study required for honors candidates, culminating in a thesis. Registration only by express approval of the program director.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/amst-american-studies/500/amst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 113","title":"Prin Soc & Cultural Anthr","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Cross-cultural approaches to the study of such topics as inequality, polity, language, economic behavior, the body, and other categorical distinctions emergent from human practice. Exposure to anthropological theory, methods, and ethnography. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/100/anthr-113","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Julie Starr"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 259","title":"Digital Tech&soc Tran","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Examines some of the ways in which digital technologies have been imagined to be important to social change, transformation, or innovation. Proponents of the use of digital technologies toward social change have focused on their speed, connectivity, and capacity. The course will introduce some of these arguments, will review some critiques of these arguments, and will suggest via ethnographic cases that digital technologies, like all sociocultural forms, should be studied with careful attention to contextual concerns.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/200/anthr-259","sections":[{"days":"F","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Spring 2027 Justice Lab students only.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 274","title":"Medical Anthropology","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Engaging with questions of power, knowledge, embodiment, and inequality, students will examine topics including medicalization, mental health, pharmaceuticalization, biological citizenship, structural violence, and the politics of life and death. Throughout the course, we ask how medicine both reflects and transforms the societies in which it operates. Engaging with questions of power, knowledge, embodiment, and inequality, students will examine topics including medicalization, mental health, pharmaceuticalization, biological citizenship, structural violence, and the politics of life and death. Throughout the course, we ask how medicine both reflects and transforms the societies in which it operates.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/200/anthr-274","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Milica Kolarevic"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 288","title":"The Corporation","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"The Corporation examines how corporations have evolved as economic, political, and cultural institutions, shaping and reflecting the societies in which they operate. Drawing on historical analysis and ethnographic perspectives, the course traces their development from early chartered companies to the 2008 financial crisis, exploring key transformations in corporate structure, legal personhood, and financialization. We will consider how corporations have been shaped by historical forces such as colonialism, industrialization, and neoliberalism, while also analyzing their cultural logics, organizational practices, and symbolic power. Readings will examine how corporations have reshaped labor, culture, and economic inequality, as well as the ways they have been challenged and reformed. By examining corporate influence across time and space, students will gain a deeper understanding of its role in the modern world.","cross_listed":["HIST 288"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/200/anthr-288","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 348","title":"Audiovisual Entertainment","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"This course explores how narratives and identities are constructed, mediated, and consumed through a combination of sound and image in audiovisual entertainment such as anime, cartoons, and video games. Drawing on anthropology, media studies, performance studies, and sociolinguistics, we will explore audiovisual entertainment not merely as a leisure consumption but as the means through which creative workers and fan-viewers co-operatively construe and (re)imagine social relations.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-348","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Spencer C. Chen"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 350","title":"Anthropology of Play","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"This course examines play as a serious social, cultural, and political practice. Drawing on anthropological theories of ritual, performance, materiality, affect, and infrastructure, we explore how play organizes social life across contexts, from children's games and sports to toys, anime, video games, trading cards, and digital platforms. Rather than treating play as trivial or escapist, the course approaches it as a domain through which identities are formed, communities are sustained, economies are structured, and power is negotiated.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-350","sections":[{"days":"R","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chenyu Wang","Spencer C. Chen"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 358W","title":"History of Ideas","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"A consideration of major paradigms in anthropology from the 19th century to the present. The influence of various theoretical perspectives on ethnographic and archaeological description and analysis.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-358","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nathan B. Goodale","Chenyu Wang"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 370","title":"Sociolinguistics Globaliztn","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Explores the relationship between language variation and change, on the one hand, and the movement of sound and image in the wake of social and political economic processes variously identified as globalization, on the other hand. Of special concern are the ways in which processes of globalization are mediated by institutional and national forms.","cross_listed":["EDUC 370"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/300/anthr-370","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 412","title":"Food Justice Mohawk Valley","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"Students will grapple with the inherent tension between the fact that there are people who live in the area that don't have enough to eat and the way that all attempts to mitigate social problems are inevitably informed by certain kinds of cultural/economic/political conceptual frameworks that often remain unexamined and/or invisible. As we work our way through the course readings, the students will be asked to pair up with a local organization that is working to alleviate food-related problems in the area. As they work with the organizations, the students will be tasked with examining the successes of, and the difficulties faced by, the institutions, including the ways that people complicate any implementation of a solution.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/400/anthr-412","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Julie Starr"]}]},
{"code":"ANTHR 501","title":"Sr Thesis Cultural Anthr","department":"Anthropology","credits":1,"description":"The research process as it relates to the fulfillment of the senior project, including the revision of the draft created during the senior seminar and extension of cultural analysis. Honors in the concentration partly depends on an A- or higher in the course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/anthr-anthropology/500/anthr-501","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Spencer C. Chen"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 116","title":"Second Term Arabic","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"Continued study with emphasis on verbal proficiency, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities. Prerequisites, Arabic 115 or placement.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/100/arabc-116","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 210","title":"Minorities in the Middle East","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"This course explores how minority communities in the Middle East-Druze, ?Alawites, Kurds, Yezidis, Armenians, Maronites, Copts, Assyrians, and others-have been formed and transformed from the late Ottoman era to the present. Beginning with the shift from imperial pluralism to nation-state \"minorities,\" it examines how religion, identity, nationalism, and political power shape communal identity and minority-state relations. Case studies span Lebanon, Syria, Israel, Turkey, Iran, Iraq, and Egypt, addressing genocide, displacement, nationalism, citizenship, security, and diaspora. Drawing on history, anthropology, religious studies, and political science, students examine how communities negotiate identity, power, and recognition in a plural Middle East. This course examines historical and contemporary experiences of minority communities in the Middle East. Some course materials address difficult subjects, including genocide, mass violence, persecution, forced displacement, sexual violence, religious and ethnic discrimination, and war. These topics are approached in their historical and scholarly contexts and are essential to understanding the experiences of the communities studied in the course. (Location)","cross_listed":["MEIWS 210","RELST 210"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/200/arabc-210","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 216W","title":"Fourth Term Arabic","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"Advanced level study with emphasis on verbal proficiency, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with supplemental Language Center projects and activities. Short readings from authentic sources.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/200/arabc-216","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"ARABC 315","title":"Advanced Arabic","department":"Arabic","credits":1,"description":"Advanced Arabic is a course designed for students who have completed four semesters of Arabic. The course will help students move from Intermediate Middle level to Advanced. Students will focus on acquiring more vocabulary and more knowledge of the fundamental grammatical and morphological structures of the language. They will also learn about the literary traditions of the Arab World through readings of abridged literary works. The instructor will select the literary work and prepare all the material used for the class. This course is sponsored by the New York Six Liberal Arts Consortium, from an award by the Andrew W. Mellon Foundation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arabc-arabic/300/arabc-315","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["NY6 Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Mireille G. Koukjian"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 106","title":"Principles of Archaeology","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the fundamentals of archaeology, with emphasis on human biological and cultural records. Topics include a review of archaeological field methods such as sampling, survey and excavation, and analytic methods such as dating, typology and formation processes. Three hours of class with lab exercises embedded within that time. Occasionally two sections of this course are offered.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/100/arch-106","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Daiana Rivas-Tello"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 229","title":"Archaeology of Death","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"While death is a human universal, the ways societies deal with death, bodies, and burial vary greatly. This course explores mortuary practices through the archaeological record and what they can tell us about ritual, social, economic, and ideological institutions in the past.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/200/arch-229","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Eric Hubbard"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 243","title":"North American Archaeology","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"The history of Native American cultural development prior to European contact.Topics include the timing and effects of human entry into North America, ice-age adaptations, plant and animal domestication, agriculture and beginnings of complex societies. This course will also consider the contemporary debates archaeologists grapple with when interpreting the region's deeper history.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/200/arch-243","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":"2012","types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Daiana Rivas-Tello"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 248","title":"Archaeology of Water","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"Water is many things at once: a basic biological necessity, a sacred substance, and a source of political power. Today, water crises reshape lives around the world, from droughts and vanishing aquifers in North America and West Asia to the rising coastlines threatening communities in the Gulf Coast and Southeast Asia. This course examines multiple themes on water through archaeological and anthropological case studies, approaches, and concepts. We'll look at how humans have exploited, managed, and made meaning out of water for the last several millennia, from the earliest farming and herding societies to current concerns on climate and AI infrastructure.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/200/arch-248","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"2012","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Eric Hubbard"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 254","title":"Crafting Culture","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"How can crafting shape societies? This course examines how crafting is not only the production of handmade goods but also a deeply social practice embedded within geological landscapes, belief systems, and political economies. Drawing on key concepts in archaeology, anthropology, Indigenous studies, art history, and museum studies, students will analyze archaeological and ethnographic case studies on crafting practices (e.g., pottery production, textile weaving, beadwork) across various cultural contexts.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/200/arch-254","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"2012","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"2012","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Daiana Rivas-Tello"]}]},
{"code":"ARCH 511","title":"Sr Thesis Archaeology","department":"Archaeology","credits":1,"description":"Continuation of participation in Archaeology 551 with revision and expansion of the senior thesis. Honors in the concentration is partly dependent on an A- or better in the course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arch-archaeology/500/arch-511","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Nathan B. Goodale"]}]},
{"code":"ART 104","title":"Drawing","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will introduce drawing as both a tool for generating ideas, and as an art form in itself. Classes will feature experiments designed to spark and sustain creative impulses, as well as more traditional drawing exercises to train the eye and hand. This will be a multidisciplinary approach, focusing less on perfecting a craft than on building communication skills through visual language. Through discussion and critique, students will develop critical skills and learn how to convey their concepts with simple mark-making. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-104","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"101","types":["Proseminar","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"101","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mark B. Alan"]}]},
{"code":"ART 106","title":"Introduction to Ceramics","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Handbuilding techniques will be employed to explore the sculptural possibilities of clay. A developed visual and conceptual vocabulary will accompany the technical aspects of ceramics through studio practice and class critique. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-106","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"116","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph, and Juniors","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"116","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Rebecca S. Murtaugh"]}]},
{"code":"ART 107","title":"Art & the Environment","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Three-dimensional explorations will focus and reflect upon our relationship with the natural and constructed world. Emphasis on process and the language of materials will direct form, meaning, and the context of objects and installations created. A developed visual and conceptual vocabulary will accompany studio practice and class critique.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-107","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"116","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"116","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Rebecca S. Murtaugh"]}]},
{"code":"ART 109","title":"Introduction to Sculpture","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will provide extensive opportunities to develop observational, conceptual and technical skills in sculpture. Basic woodworking, assemblage, casting, carving and installation methods will be covered, while examining the unique demands and benefits of working in three-dimensions. Through lectures, discussions and critiques, students will delve deeply into what it means to make sculpture in the 21st century. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-109","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"116","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"116","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy Brener"]}]},
{"code":"ART 113","title":"Intro to Media:integrated Med","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course will serve as an introduction to digital technology in contemporary art making as it relates to interactivity, collaboration, and emerging practices. Emphasis will be placed on hands on experimentation and exploration across disciplines. We will cover installation art, web art, digital and time-based art, and immersive environments. Students will gain an overview of emerging digital practices, collaborative production strategies and correlations to social, aesthetic and theoretical issues. Other topics may include virtuality, performance, cyborgs, and network culture.","cross_listed":["DARTS 113","MUSIC 113"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-113","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"143","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph, Jrs, and Srs","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"124","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff"]}]},
{"code":"ART 116","title":"Intro to Photography","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Fundamentals of photography through the use of both analog and digital SLR cameras, black and white darkroom techniques, film scanning and digital printing. Emphasis on development of technical and aesthetic skills, and the potential of the medium as a tool for artistic expression. Cameras will be provided by the College. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-116","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs., Soph, & Juniors","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"133","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Daniel Boardman"]}]},
{"code":"ART 160","title":"Figure Drawing","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Application of basic drawing principles to the representation of the human figure, with emphasis on anatomy and proportion. Examination of related topics such as the figure in the environment and portraiture. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/100/art-160","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"208","types":["Proseminar","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"208","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Katharine M. Kuharic"]}]},
{"code":"ART 203","title":"Introduction to Painting","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the study of the methods and techniques of oil painting, with emphasis on still-life, figures and landscape.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-203","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"207","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph., and Jrs. Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"207","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Katharine M. Kuharic"]}]},
{"code":"ART 213","title":"Introduction to Video","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to fundamentals of craft and study of experimental video. Emphasis on developing ideas and conveying meaning through video art, working outside commercial traditions. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-213","sections":[{"days":"M","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kerry Downey"]}]},
{"code":"ART 221","title":"Intro to Animation","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the history, tools and language of basic animation styles. This course will trace the history of experiments in animated imagery from 19th Century photography through 20th Century film into 21st Century digital works. The class will cover basic techniques in Photoshop, Adobe Premier, and illustration and animation software interfaces.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-221","sections":[{"days":"T","room":"144","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kerry Downey"]}]},
{"code":"ART 233","title":"Intro to Printmaking","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Exploration of printmaking's interdisciplinary nature, blending drawing and painting via traditional methods including notan, rubbings, monoprint, monotype, suminagashi, linocut, and woodcut. Production of relief prints, carved blocks, print editions and an accordion book. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/200/art-233","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"206","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Sophomores, & Juniors only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"206","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mark B. Alan"]}]},
{"code":"ART 302","title":"Adv Photo:analog Tech","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"Advanced investigation and study of the creative tools of black-and-white photography through the use of analog film cameras. Emphasis on medium and large format cameras, studio techniques and darkroom printing. Continued exploration of personal vision with emphasis on social and cultural contexts for photography.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-302","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"133","types":["Studio Art Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., and Sophomores","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"133","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"W","room":"144","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Robert B. Knight"]}]},
{"code":"ART 305","title":"Adv Paint: Figuration","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course develops basic paint handling, visual syntax, and conceptual abilities pertaining to the figure in space. Students will examine historical and contemporary methods. Students will gain an appreciation of the physicality, emotional touchstones, and communicative power of paint. Students will explore varied genres within representation. including contemporary portraiture, history painting, the nude, and self portraiture.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-305","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"207","types":["Experiential Learning","Proseminar","Studio Art Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"207","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Katharine M. Kuharic"]}]},
{"code":"ART 308","title":"Advanced Sculpture","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"This course explores form, surface and content at an advanced level, questioning how sculpture can most effectively engage the viewer's mind and body. Students will develop their own studio practices while experimenting with a broad range of techniques, including carving in wood and wax; mold-making and casting in plaster, resin, and silicone; and digital rendering and 3D printing.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/300/art-308","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"116","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"116","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Amy Brener"]}]},
{"code":"ART 502","title":"Senior Project Exhibition","department":"Art","credits":1,"description":"A course for senior concentrators in which students finalize their work from ART-501 for presentation in the Wellin Museum. Students will learn exhibition design, presentation issues for museum exhibition, installation techniques, working with galleries and museums, writing of artist statement and label text, documentation of 2D and 3D work, and basic website design.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/500/art-502","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"201","types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only.","end_time":"19:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"201","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"19:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"16:00"},{"days":"W","room":"205","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"19:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"16:00"}],"start_time":"16:00:00","instructors":["Katharine M. Kuharic","Rebecca S. Murtaugh","Mark B. Alan","Amy Brener","Kerry Downey","Robert B. Knight"]}]},
{"code":"ART 503","title":"Sr Project Fall Work Exhibit","department":"Art","credits":0,"description":"Public exhibition of thesis work from Art 501 for students not enrolled in Art 502.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/art-art/500/art-503","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Katharine M. Kuharic","Rebecca S. Murtaugh","Mark B. Alan","Amy Brener","Kerry Downey","Robert B. Knight"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 101","title":"Introduction to Art History","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This course is an introduction to the production, reception, and circulation of art in the modern world, from roughly 1450 to the present. We will examine art in its social, economic, and political context, while focusing on themes of artistic mobility and collaboration, as well as exchanges across cultures and media. Students will gain a basic understanding of visual vocabulary and literacy, and develop visual analysis skills by working with objects in campus and local collections.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-101","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Ruth and Elmer Wellin Museum","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Years","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Ruth and Elmer Wellin Museum","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery","Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 103W","title":"Art of Advertising","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Advertising images are everywhere, beckoning us to look and believe their message. But how do such images actually work, and where do their visual strategies come from? This writing-intensive seminar equips students with the foundational skills of visual and contextual analysis necessary for the study of art and visual media. We will study how artists, designers, photographers, and advertising professionals created influential campaigns in print, on television, and online. Students will learn to analyze how images and text work together, how ads capture our attention, how they borrow from different visual traditions, and how they shape our desires, often through troubling visual conventions.","cross_listed":["CNMS 103W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-103","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores only","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nadya Bair"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 152W","title":"Intersections Global Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"In this course we will look closely at 25 objects (roughly one per day) that embody significant intersections among different cultures and/or periods. We will be learning about how to look at works of art and how to effectively express our thoughts about them in spoken and written words. All class sessions will be held at the Wellin Museum of Art, Burke Special Collections, or the College Archives to guide our object-based study.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/100/arth-152","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 201","title":"Artifact Testament & Monument","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the rich art histories of Asia from the prehistoric period to the present, with an emphasis on the arts of China, Japan, Korea, India, Sri Lanka, Cambodia, and Indonesia. With the study of select objects, paintings, sculptures, monuments, and associated social, religious, and political milieus, students will be introduced to major styles and traditions in the history of art across Asia and will engage with critical themes in the interpretation of Asian art histories.","cross_listed":["ASNST 201"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 212","title":"Black Conceptualism","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"From the 1960s to the present, Black artists have played a leading role in developing the experimental techniques of conceptual art. Across the Americas, Europe, and Africa, Black artists moved beyond traditional mediums like painting and sculpture to create artworks in the form of typewritten texts, logical systems, philosophical treatises, historical archives, video installations, and street performances. These methods were tied to a wide range of themes: the critique of racist institutions, research into suppressed histories, explorations of postcolonial and diasporic identity, and the embodied experiences of gender and sexuality. Readings will be drawn from art history, artists' writings, and interdisciplinary scholarship in Black Studies.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-212","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Mitchell Herrmann"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 274","title":"Building Jerusalem","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Since its foundation, the city of Jerusalem has been claimed materially and spiritually by Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. This course will, through its art and architecture, trace the complex history of Jerusalem from ca. 957 BCE to today's divided city. The art and architecture of the city is contextualized in a way that speaks beyond the often-used and simplistic tripartite manner of constructing identity and history in the city.","cross_listed":["JWST 274","MEIWS 274"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-274","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 284","title":"Northern Renaissance Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the distinctive ways in which art was crafted and consumed in northern Europe during the age of Renaissance and Reformation. We will examine paintings and prints, propaganda and princely splendor, and carved altarpieces and ceremonial armor against the backdrop of both city and court, while considering issues of religious function, social use, and the economic history of the arts. The course also fosters a critical awareness of the methods of art history by drawing attention to scholarship on Northern art that has, in many ways, laid the foundations for modernity.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-284","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 296","title":"Global Contemporary Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Global Contemporary Art surveys the expanding definitions, contexts, and forms of art in the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, paying particular attention to the significance of artistic agency, artists' writings, globalization, the art market, and political engagement and social transformation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/200/arth-296","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mitchell Herrmann"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 301","title":"Avant Garde Cinema","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"A history of alternatives to commercial movies, focusing on surrealist and dadaist film, visual music, psychodrama, direct cinema, the film society movement, personal cinema, the New American Cinema, structuralism, Queer cinema, feminist cinema, minor cinema, recycled cinema and devotional cinema. While conventional entertainment films use the novel, the short story and the stage drama as their primary instigations, experimental and avant-garde films are analogous to music, poetry, painting, sculpture and collage. Not open to first-year students.","cross_listed":["CNMS 301","LIT 301"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-301","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, and Sophomores. First Years by instructor permission.","end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 318","title":"Epic Tales","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"South Asia's extensive corpus of epics, fables, popular tales, and romances have found spirited visual expression in the region's history of art and architecture. This seminar will analyze artistic choice and the role that it plays in how words are visually translated into images. We will examine a range of case studies from a period spanning over two thousand years to understand how artists shaped space, unfurled time, and materialized heroes and villains in painting and sculpture.","cross_listed":["ASNST 318"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-318","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 336","title":"Anthropocene Aesthetics","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"This seminar in contemporary art history and theory explores ecological art in the era of anthropogenic climate change. The course's guiding question will be: what forms might an aesthetic experience of nature take when it no longer privileges the human observer, but rather cultivates an equality and reciprocity among all forms of life? Possible answers will be drawn from recent work in art history, Black studies, Indigenous studies, continental philosophy, and science and technology studies. The course's topics will include: post-Enlightenment aesthetics of nature, biopower, vitalism, post- and anti-humanisms, plant philosophies, bacteria and fungi studies, and deep time. These theoretical frameworks will be complemented with case studies on a global array of contemporary artists. Not open to first year students.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-336","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Mitchell Herrmann"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 345","title":"Postcol Theory & Islamic Art","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"How does the study of art and architectural history interface with colonial, postcolonial, and neocolonial structures of power? This is a pressing question for those who study the visual cultures of the Middle East, a region in which these power structures have had considerable impact historically as well as in the contemporary moment. This class will consider from theoretical and practical vantage points how colonial, postcolonial, and neocolonial systems and institutions have governed or influenced the methods of study, scope, and modes of display of historical art from the Middle East. Topics will include colonial-era collection and museum practices, Orientalism and its critics, post-colonial nationalist art historical discourses, cultural heritage in and during the 'wars on terror', and visual discourses of contemporary Islamophobia. Students may pursue research projects with objects or items from the Wellin Museum and/or the Michael Lang '67 Special Collections and Archives.","cross_listed":["MEIWS 345"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/300/arth-345","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"ARTH 493W","title":"Senior Project in Art History","department":"Art History","credits":1,"description":"Examination of selected topics in Art History culminating in the Senior Project. Topics vary according to interests of students. Includes the development of the Senior Project, from topic selection and bibliography development to reviews of research skills and conceptual and theoretical frameworks resulting in a final essay or project formally presented before the Department. Open to senior concentrators only, or by consent of the instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/arth-art-history/400/arth-493","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only. Restricted to Sr. Art History Major","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Nadya Bair"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Arathi Menon"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 143W","title":"Sacred in South Asia","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"What constitutes the sacred in south Asia? Is it a person, place, river, hill, temple or nature/ecology? Where and how did the notion of sacrality emerge in South Asia? Is it linked exclusively to religious institutions or is it found in the daily lives of people? This course will examine these questions by exploring the multiple religious traditions of South Asia and examining their essential beliefs, doctrines, institutions, rituals and popular practices through a study of texts, material culture, films and ethnographic accounts.","cross_listed":["RELST 143W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-143","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to 1st Years, Sophomores, and Juniors","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Abhishek S. Amar"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 161W","title":"Sages of the Tao in China","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the two most significant sages in Chinese history and the disciples and schools that traced their origins to them. Close readings of texts attributed to Laozi and Confucius, the conflicting interpretations of their teachings throughout Chinese history, and the proliferation of schools that claimed to transmit their Tao. An eminent historian once said, \"Every era has its own Confucius; it's just that there are many different Confucius[es] in any one era.\" This adage, as we shall see, applies to Laozi as well.","cross_listed":["HIST 161W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/100/asnst-161","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, and 1st Yrs","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Thomas A. Wilson"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 201","title":"Artifact Testament & Monument","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the rich art histories of Asia from the prehistoric period to the present, with an emphasis on the arts of China, Japan, Korea, India, Sri Lanka, Cambodia, and Indonesia. With the study of select objects, paintings, sculptures, monuments, and associated social, religious, and political milieus, students will be introduced to major styles and traditions in the history of art across Asia and will engage with critical themes in the interpretation of Asian art histories.","cross_listed":["ARTH 201"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/200/asnst-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 232","title":"Resistance History Thru Film","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Through films and documentaries about places with contentious histories and ongoing political conflicts, this course will examine the solidarities and fractures that resistance against colonial and foreign control brings about in struggling societies. The course will particularly explore the anticolonial struggles of Palestinians, Algerians, Irish, Kurds, Kashmiris, Tamils, and Indigenous Americans, among others, and in doing so engage with themes of sovereignty and self-determination, nationality and belonging, and history and memory. Such inquiries not only have real-world stakes for peoples' survival and political identities, but also shape in profound ways the imaginations and practices of resistance and solidarity across the globe.","cross_listed":["CNMS 232","HIST 232","MEIWS 232"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/200/asnst-232","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 271","title":"Mother Nature","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"How have people in India understood and interacted with mother nature? How do they conceptualize the environment and ecosystems? How do they deal with climate change and ecological disasters? What role does religion play in shaping human attitude to the mother nature? Focusing on Hinduism, Buddhism, and Jainism, the course examines how Indian communities have engaged and negotiated their relationships with the natural world to consider themes including the conception of environment as sacred space, ethical living, non-violence, animal-protection, and food-culture. No prior coursework on Religion or Asia is required.","cross_listed":["RELST 271"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/200/asnst-271","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Corbett Costello"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 289","title":"Gunpowder Islam","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Fifty years ago, the scholar Marshall Hodgson coined the term \"gunpowder empires\" to describe the introduction of new military technology by early modern Islamic kingdoms across Eurasia, specifically in Turkey, Iran, and India. Using this cultural framework to study the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal Empires, this course will focus on issues of imperial power and expansion, social and religious authority, protest and dissent, language and literature, law and ethics, paintings and architecture, as well as gender and sexuality. We will explore the historiography of these Islamic polities along with reading primary sources including court chronicles, local histories, political treatises, religious texts, and poetry in Turkish, Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Hindavi and other languages.","cross_listed":["HIST 289","MDRST 289","MEIWS 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/200/asnst-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 290","title":"Global Asia","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"This methods course is designed to introduce students to the theoretical and conceptual frameworks to study Asia. This course will adopt an interdisciplinary and comparative lens to study multiple regions, cultures, and histories of East, South and West Asia. How are the particularities of Asian identity reflected in visual cultures? In what ways have scholars evaluated textual and material sources to study different Asian religions such as Buddhism, Islam, Hinduism and Confucianism? How has the colonial experience shaped periodization debates, modernity, and everyday life in countries like India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and Myanmar? We will examine the diversity of Asian languages and literatures in translation, as well as global and transregional connections evident in travelogues, trade networks, and musical exchanges.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/200/asnst-290","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Abhishek S. Amar","Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 318","title":"Epic Tales","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"South Asia's extensive corpus of epics, fables, popular tales, and romances have found spirited visual expression in the region's history of art and architecture. This seminar will analyze artistic choice and the role that it plays in how words are visually translated into images. We will examine a range of case studies from a period spanning over two thousand years to understand how artists shaped space, unfurled time, and materialized heroes and villains in painting and sculpture.","cross_listed":["ARTH 318"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/300/asnst-318","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Arathi Menon"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 346W","title":"Madness, Lovesickness, & Jinn","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"\"Madness\" evokes images of rambling, potentially violent, individuals on the margins of society. How did historical attitudes to insanity evolve in early modern and modern Eurasia? Were madpeople always ostracized or were they simply objects of sympathy and pity? What did hospitals and other networks of care and healing look like for mentally ill patients? In poetry, the pain of separation of the lover from their beloved produced lovesickness. Finally, demonic possession of the brain by jinns (Islamic spirits) was thought to cause madness. How can we understand the literary, religious and supernatural world of these actors without dismissing it? This course will take a holistic view of insanity while engaging with the rise of asylums or other lunatic institutions.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/300/asnst-346","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores. First Years require instructor permission.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"ASNST 550","title":"Senior Project","department":"Asian Studies","credits":1,"description":"Seminar in which concentrators develop individually-designed research projects in consultation with the instructor and one other member of the Asian Studies Program Committee. Students discuss their ongoing research with their peers throughout the semester, culminating in formal presentations of the final projects, which demonstrate mastery of interdisciplinary approaches to the study of Asia. Prerequisites, at least one Asian Studies course offered at the 300-level.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/asnst-asian-studies/500/asnst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Asian Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors only","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Abhishek S. Amar"]}]},
{"code":"BICHM 270","title":"Biological Chemistry","department":"Biochemistry","credits":1,"description":"A survey of the chemical and physical nature of biological macromolecules, including nucleic acids, proteins, lipids and carbohydrates; biochemistry of enzyme catalysis; bioenergetics and regulatory mechanisms. Principles and techniques of experimental biochemistry, focusing on isolation methods and techniques for analyzing structure and function. This course satisfies the second semester of a one-year General Chemistry requirement for post-graduate Health Professions programs, however, this course might not also satisfy a Health Profession program's requirement for a course in Biochemistry.Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":["BIO 270","CHEM 270"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bichm-biochemistrymolecular-biology/200/bichm-270","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Ryan J. Martinie"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Michael A. Welsh"]}]},
