{"code":"BIO 100P","title":"Exb: Evolution of Sex","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"A thematic course exploring five fundamental features of all biological systems, including structure and function, information flow, energy and matter, interactions, and evolution. How did sexual reproduction evolve? Why don't all animals make clonal copies of themselves? How does sex shape selection and the evolution of plants and animals? How does sex influence human life and society? These are some of the questions this course will explore as we dive into understanding the origins of sexual reproduction across the animal tree of life. Students must concurrently enroll in a BIO 100L lab section. Not open to students who have taken any other Explorations in Biology course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/100/bio-100p","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":30,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, Sophomores, & 1st Years. Not open to those who took BIO 100*. Must also sign up for a BIO-100L section.","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Ariel F. Kahrl"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 200","title":"Microscopy Fundamentals","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"This course is a broad introduction to many common types of microscopy (bright field, confocal microscopy, electron microscopy). In addition to learning the theoretical foundations, students will also get hands-on practice with microscopes throughout the semester. The course will also cover the basics of digital image acquisition, processing, and analysis. Three hours a week, split between class and lab.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-200","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kyle S. Martin"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 203","title":"Biomaterials","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"Plants and animals create materials to accomplish their complex goals for survival and reproduction. These biological materials, from wood to silk to shells to fur, have diverse and unique physical properties. This course will explore the physical/engineering properties of biological materials. We will learn the basics of structural mechanics while asking how and why organisms create these materials.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-203","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Kyle S. Martin"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 210","title":"Ecology & Evolutionary Bio","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"An intermediate course that provides a comprehensive introduction to the fundamental principles of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology, emphasizing their deep interconnectedness. Students will explore the mechanisms of ecology and evolution, spanning from molecular-level changes to population, community, and ecosystem phenomena. Students will develop analytical and quantitative tools to solve real-world ecological and evolutionary problems. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory. Not open to those who took BIO 237. Must also register for BIO 210 lab section.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-210","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Pete Guiden"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 210L","title":"Ecol. & Evolutionary Bio Lab","department":"Biology","credits":0,"description":null,"cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-210","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LAB","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Pete Guiden"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 211","title":"Genes, Molecules, & Cells","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"An intermediate course that examines genetics and biochemistry within the context of the cell. This course will focus on the macromolecules of life, genetic material and classical genetics, fundamental biochemical pathways, and cellular structure and function. A particular focus will be placed on proteins and nucleic acids and how cells use these molecules to direct the mechanisms of inheritance, energy production, and cellular reproduction. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory.","cross_listed":["NEURO 211"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/200/bio-211","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":30,"comments":"Open to Sophomores and 1st Yrs only. Not open to those who took BIO 248 or BIO 249. Must also register for BIO 211 lab section.","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Rhea R. Datta"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":30,"comments":"Open to Sophomores and 1st Yrs only. Not open to those who took BIO 248. Must also register for BIO 211 lab section.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Ivan Bochkov"]}]},
