{"code":"BIO 311W","title":"Evolution","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"This course will cover the fundamental principles of evolution starting with the processes that generate variation within and between populations and building up to the patterns that result in speciation and macroevolution. Students will spend the semester learning this material while engaging in research projects that involve data analysis, visual and oral communication, and extensive scientific writing.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/300/bio-311","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Ariel F. Kahrl"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 325","title":"Immunology","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"This course introduces the cellular and molecular mechanisms of vertebrate immunity in health and disease. Topics include the evolution and organization of the immune system, innate and adaptive immune responses, immune cell development and communication, and the dysregulation and therapeutic manipulation of immune responses. Lectures, case studies, and primary literature will be used to explore fundamental immunological concepts and their applications to human health. Laboratory experiments will examine immune cell function and introduce current immunological techniques through guided exercises and independent projects.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/300/bio-325","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LAB","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Elena Tonc"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 330","title":"Systems Neuroscience","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"The primary focus of this course is on the physiological and chemical basis of behavior from a systems perspective. Topics include analysis of sensory and motor systems; motivated behaviors; stress, anxiety and mental illness; and learning and memory. Laboratory exercises introduce students to the anatomy and physiology of the mammalian central nervous system and to some of the principal techniques used in systems and behavioral neuroscience. Three hours of class and three hours of laboratory. Does not count toward the lab requirement in Psychology.","cross_listed":["NEURO 330","PSYCH 330"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/300/bio-330","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":10,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"T","room":"3087","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Abigail K. Myers"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":10,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"},{"days":"W","room":"3087","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Abigail K. Myers"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 427","title":"Sem in Disease & Behavior","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"The relationship between animal behavior and disease is bidirectional: behavior can affect the likelihood of acquiring a disease, and disease can subsequently alter behavior. In this class, we examine the fundamentals of animal behavior (e.g., sexual selection, kin selection, social evolution, communication and signals, reproductive strategies) and apply them to current research on the ecology and evolution of disease. Discussion of foundational readings and the primary literature.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/400/bio-427","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Andrea K. Townsend"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 429","title":"Sem in Cancer Biology","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"Why have we not cured cancer yet? There are many good ways to answer the question. By the end of this course, you will be able to articulate a dozen valid explanations and reframe these challenges as future research, healthcare, and policy goals. Through analysis and discussion of recent primary literature, we will explore the complex genetics and epigenetics of the group of diseases we call malignant neoplasms. Topics include mutations that drive tumor progression, phenotypes of malignant cells, interactions of cancer cells with their environment, techniques for studying cancer biology, and therapies used in modern oncology.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/400/bio-429","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Ivan Bochkov"]}]},
{"code":"BIO 443","title":"Sem Bioinformatics","department":"Biology","credits":1,"description":"Study of computer-based approaches to molecular investigations: sequence variation, molecular evolution, functional and comparative genomics, and computational biology. Both literature-based lecture and training on the use of bioinformatic software are included.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/bio-biology/400/bio-443","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., & Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Wei-Jen Chang"]}]},
