{"code":"ECON 275","title":"Microeconomic Theory","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"The theory of consumer behavior. Theories of the firm and market structures, and of resource allocation, pricing and income distribution. General equilibrium and economic efficiency.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/200/econ-275","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Paul A. Hagstrom"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Paul A. Hagstrom"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 285","title":"Macroeconomic Theory","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Theories of business cycles and economic growth. Theories of monetary policy, budget and trade balances, aggregate consumption and investment activity, unemployment, inflation, technological change and productivity growth.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/200/econ-285","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Hannah Rhodenhiser"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Hannah Rhodenhiser"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Christophre Georges"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":27,"comments":"Not open to Sr ECON concentrators","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Christophre Georges"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 318W","title":"Econ Tech & Innovation","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"An examination of the nature and implications of innovation and technological change. We will investigate the history and economic theory of innovation and technological progress, related policy debates, and future prospects. Particular attention to the implications of recent developments in information technology and robotics for labor markets and the distribution of economic wellbeing.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-318","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Christophre Georges"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 364","title":"Financial Crises","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"An analysis of economic and financial crises including the Panic of 1907, the Great Depression, the 1997 Asian Financial Crisis, and the Global Financial Crisis. For each crisis, we will explore the background leading up to the crisis, how the crisis unfolded, how policymakers responded, and the long lasting impact of the crisis.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-364","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Cody A. Couture"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 365","title":"American Econ History","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"An examination and explanation of the development of the American economy, focusing on the period from 1840 through World War II. Topics include the economics of slavery and share cropping, the rise of big business, railroads and economic growth, the development of banks and the causes of the Great Depression.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-365","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Jian Qi Tan"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 379","title":"Pol Econ of Env Policy","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Markets, individual preferences, and political structures all play a key role in shaping environmental and energy policy. In this course, we will adopt a multi-level political economy approach that draws on behavioral economics to understand individual environmental preferences and works upward through electoral politics and institutional structures. Students will work with economic theory to understand predicted policy effects, and polling and congressional voting data to understand the factors that shape environmental policy.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-379","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"This course is part of the Spring 2027 Innovation Co-Lab","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Jeffrey R. Cross"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 380","title":"Environmental Economics","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"An examination of issues in environmental policy from the perspective of economic theory. Topics include the measurement of benefits and costs of curtailing pollution and preserving ecosystems, the design of public policies to improve environmental quality, and the examination of past and current environmental programs in the United States and their success. Also considers sustainable growth and issues of environmental equity.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/300/econ-380","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Case Tatro"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 420","title":"Economics of Education","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A study of issues in the economics of education, using microeconomic theory and empirical methods to analyze K-12 education policy. Topics include: the determinants of school quality, how to find and retain effective teachers, the effects of school accountability programs on student achievement, the role of charter schools and school choice in the provision of high-quality public education, and the consequences of more widespread early childhood education. Students will learn to read and critique empirical research to understand its implications for policy, focusing on the experimental and quasi-experimental methods most commonly used by applied microeconomists.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-420","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Hannah Ruebeck"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 421","title":"Development Economics","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Students will learn about some of the challenges faced by people in developing countries, and about the tools used to assess the interventions designed to confront them. The course will be mostly empirical - students will critically read research papers and learned about the methods frequently used by applied microeconomists.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-421","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Emily Conover"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 425","title":"Financial Economics","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A study of individual level investment decisions and the equilibrium determination of asset prices. Mean-variance analysis motivated by the tradeoff between risk and return. An introduction to asset pricing models, including the CAPM and multi-factor models. An introduction to derivatives, including stock options, futures and swaps. Discussions of the Efficient Markets Hypothesis, arbitrage, and contributions from behavioral finance. Other topics may include: fixed income pricing, Arrow-Debreu securities and the completeness of markets, and the binomial asset pricing model.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/400/econ-425","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., and Sophomores","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Cody A. Couture"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 505","title":"Sr Proj in Econ Development","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A senior project for concentrators in the department who are not pursuing honors. Students will work on projects related to the development economics. Open to concentrators only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/500/econ-505","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Senior ECON Concentrators only. Instructor's signature required.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Emily Conover"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 516","title":"Sr Project in Macro-finance","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A senior project for concentrators in the department who are not pursuing honors. Students will work on projects related to financial economics, macro-finance, or central bank policy.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/500/econ-516","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only. Restricted to Econ Concentrators. Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Cody A. Couture"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 520","title":"Sr Proj Econ of Education","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"A senior project for concentrators in the department who are not pursuing honors. Students will work on projects related to the economics of education. Open to concentrators only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/500/econ-520","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Hannah Ruebeck"]}]},
{"code":"ECON 560","title":"Research Seminar","department":"Economics","credits":1,"description":"Each student works intensively on a topic chosen in consultation with the instructor. Weekly meetings held to hear progress reports and to discuss research techniques pertinent to student topics. Candidates for honors must complete this course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/econ-economics/500/econ-560","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":6,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Hannah Ruebeck"]},{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":6,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Promise Kamanga"]},{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":6,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Case Tatro"]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 102","title":"Issues in Education","department":"Education","credits":1,"description":"What is education? What is school? How do we critically understand education and schools? What are the possibilities and limitations of education for change and justice? Emphasis on, but not limited to, American education. Includes lecture, discussion, multi-media projects, and small-group interaction.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/educ-education-studies/100/educ-102","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 203","title":"Ethnography & Education","department":"Education","credits":1,"description":"This course provides an understanding of the importance of ethnography to the consideration of issues introduced in EDUC-102. What is ethnography as a methodology for exploring education? Students will practice methods such as participant observation, interviews, and oral history through in-class exercises, as well as conduct an ethnographic project of their own.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/educ-education-studies/200/educ-203","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Julie Starr"]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 222","title":"Pov: You're Not the Main Char","department":"Education","credits":1,"description":"What does it mean to listen to someone you disagree with? Why do some conversations bring people together while others deepen division? And can we learn to disagree without dehumanizing one another? In a time of polarization, cultural conflict, and competing ideas about belonging, this course explores dialogue as a practice for navigating difference and strengthening democratic life. Drawing on intergroup dialogue and critical theories of identity, power, and socialization, students examine how our experiences shape what we believe and how we understand others. Through storytelling, dialogue, reflection, and engagement with contested issues, students will learn to listen deeply, ask better questions, navigate discomfort and conflict, and connect interpersonal experiences to larger social systems. Ultimately, the course asks: If we understand one another differently, what becomes possible?","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/educ-education-studies/200/educ-222","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Koboul E. Mansour"]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 370","title":"Sociolinguistics Globaliztn","department":"Education","credits":1,"description":"Explores the relationship between language variation and change, on the one hand, and the movement of sound and image in the wake of social and political economic processes variously identified as globalization, on the other hand. Of special concern are the ways in which processes of globalization are mediated by institutional and national forms.","cross_listed":["ANTHR 370"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/educ-education-studies/300/educ-370","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Chaise Ladousa"]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 379","title":"Lifespan Development","department":"Education","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the science of lifespan development, from conception and prenatal development to older age and death. Focuses on multiple domains of development, including cognitive, social, physical, and emotional, with emphasis on how development in all domains is shaped by both biological and social/cultural influences (e.g., gender norms, social class, stereotyping and prejudice).","cross_listed":["PSYCH 379"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/educ-education-studies/300/educ-379","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"EDUC 395N","title":"Intensive Special Needs Teach","department":"Education","credits":2,"description":"Each student is assigned full-time teaching responsibilities, under supervision, in a setting with learners with intensive special needs. Includes extensive practicum experience with a focus on teaching and case management. Papers and attendance at weekly seminars required. Course available to students enrolled in the cooperative program at the New England Center for Children; earns two course credits with only one course credit counting toward requirements for the minor in education studies. Evaluated Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":5,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 110","title":"Intro to Environmental Studie","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course presents the core literature, theories, and concepts in environmental studies, with a focus on the emerging discipline of sustainability science. It will provide a comprehensive foundation for interdisciplinary study of contemporary environmental issues that will prepare students for more advanced work on the biophysical, social and human dimensions of environmental problems, including problems at the food-energy-water-climate nexus. This course is a required component of the Environmental Studies concentration.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/100/envst-110","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Aaron L. Strong"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 151W","title":"Global History of Oil","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"How was our current dependence on petroleum created, and how can historical thinking help us imagine a better future? This course combines approaches from environmental and social history to examine the oil industry from the 19th century to today. Students explore social and environmental justice movements, the lives of oil industry workers, and our changing global economy, emphasizing peoples and places that are often marginalized. Contribute to contemporary debates on fossil fuels by analyzing museum exhibits in a final writing project.","cross_listed":["HIST 151W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/100/envst-151","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 234","title":"Environmental Justice","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This interdisciplinary seminar examines environmental justice as a movement and concept. We interrogate and discuss the historical role of racism, heteropatriarchy, colonialism and globalization in (re)producing and justifying place-based environmental contamination and toxicity. Through close analysis of case studies in the US and the Global South, students analyze and present on how disenfranchised groups have made political demands, scaled up, forged solidarities, asserted climate justice, challenged state and corporate projects and conducted citizen science in rural and urban settings.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/200/envst-234","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Aalayna R. Green"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Aalayna R. Green"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 285","title":"Intro Environmental Politics","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"An overview of environmental politics, domestic and global. Topics include the environmental movement and its history and values, anti-environmentalism, environmental policy analysis, the relation between environmental science and politics, the domestic and international environmental policy processes, the North-South debate, globalization, race and environmental justice, and the implications of environmental politics for liberal democracy. Students will explore these topics directly and through selected policy issues, including forest politics, sprawl and climate change. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["GOVT 285"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/200/envst-285","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Peter F. Cannavo"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 290","title":"Nature & Technology","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the sometimes contentious relationship between the natural world and human attempts to understand it (science) and control it (technology). We survey ethical, social, artistic and scientific distinctions between the natural world and the human-built world. Specific topics include everyday tools (e.g., hammers), food and agricultural practices (corn & chickens), modes of transportation (trains), and emerging biotechnologies (genes & humans). Readings will draw from works in philosophy of technology, environmental history, and science and technology studies.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/200/envst-290","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Wyatt J. Galusky"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 291","title":"Environmental Studies Resear","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":0.25,"description":"Independent research under the supervision of a faculty member in environmental studies. May be repeated for credit. One-quarter, one-half or one credit per semester. Open to all class years. For seniors, independent research projects should not be on the same topic as their senior thesis or capstone projects. Instructor's permission required.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/200/envst-291","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Aaron L. Strong"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Heather A. Kropp"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 310","title":"Sem: Indigenous Ecologies","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This interdisciplinary seminar explores the traditional ecological knowledge (TEK) of indigenous peoples. Drawing upon scholarship from such diverse fields as acoustic ecology, ethno-ecology, ethnography, geography, environmental history, Native American and Indigenous Studies, and religious studies, we will examine indigenous knowledge about particular species and relationships between them.","cross_listed":["AMST 310"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-310","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["J. Seth T. Schermerhorn"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 325","title":"Environmental Data Science","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course will focus on the application of statistical programming for big data associated with ongoing environmental issues. Students will gain experience in statistical programming throughout the entire data life cycle including data management and provenance, analysis, visualization, and communication. Students will learn the fundamentals of applying statistical modelling and machine learning for making predictions and inferences for environmental data. Students will also learn considerations of data science that are unique to environmental data including spatial, temporal, ethical, and justice concerns. Environmental topics will include climate change, pollution, natural disasters, and agricultural impacts.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-325","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":15,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Heather A. Kropp"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 326","title":"Internatl Conservation Policy","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This seminar course examines both the policy decisions and social justice issues that drive human actions and responses to international conservation challenges.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-326","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Aalayna R. Green"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 327","title":"Innovations in Envmntl Policy","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"This experiential course examines barriers and bottlenecks to effective environmental and energy policy implementation and the proposed solutions and potential pathways to address them. Through case studies of climate, energy, water and conservation policy implementation, the course highlights the conditions under which innovation occurs and what the factors are that make it successful. Students meet frequently with local, state and federal policy practitioners, environmental and energy advocates and technologists. The course involves field trips to both Washington, D.C. and Boston to meet with environmental policy innovators.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-327","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"This course is part of the Spring 2027 Co-Lab. Open to Co-Lab students only.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Aaron L. Strong"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 342","title":"Sociology of Climate Action","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"In the face of the ongoing climate crisis, we are also witnessing deployment of climate solutions: electrification of transportation, renewable energy development, reforestation initiatives, and reductions in animal agriculture. How do communities react to these approaches to climate action? We unpack theoretical and on-the-ground perspectives on what climate change means among different groups of people and explore people's social location influences how they understand and respond to it individually and collectively. We examine how colonialism, and macroeconomic structures shape perspectives on the experience of the climate crisis and responses to it. Students apply their theoretical understanding in structured community conversations about climate solutions with community stakeholders.","cross_listed":["SOC 342"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-342","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Experiential Learning"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Aaron L. Strong","Jaime L. Kucinskas"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 347","title":"Environ Chem and Lit","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"An exploration of how environmental chemistry shapes environmental literature. Course material will include primary chemical sources as well as essays, novels, short stories, film, and other environmental literature. This course will look at both natural environmental chemistry as well as the chemistry of anthropogenic changes and compounds, such as pesticides. Students will interrogate and discuss these connections then use them to discover their own intersections with chemistry and literature.","cross_listed":["CHEM 347"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/300/envst-347","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Carolyn P. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 520","title":"Senior Capstone Practicum","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"The course is summative and fulfills the Senior Program requirement in Environmental Studies. Building on previous coursework in the concentration, the capstone practicum focuses on developing and applying interdisciplinary research methods, skills and analytical tools to collaboratively understand and address environmental problems. Students conduct thematically linked, community-engaged research projects around a chosen salient environmental problem. Enrollment restricted to senior Environmental Studies concentrators. ENVST Program (Spring 2021: Strong and Chandrasekaran). Prerequisites, Enrollment restricted to senior environmental studies concentrators.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/500/envst-520","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only. Restricted to ENVST Concentrators.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Kropp"]}]},
{"code":"ENVST 550","title":"Senior Project","department":"Environmental Studies","credits":1,"description":"An independent study developed in consultation with a faculty advisor and the environmental studies advisory committee to explore in detail an environmental topic, culminating in a substantial research paper and oral presentation. Proposals for Senior Projects are developed with a faculty advisor and submitted to the ES advisory committee prior to course registration.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/envst-environmental-studies/500/envst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Peter F. Cannavo"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Aaron L. Strong"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Heather A. Kropp"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Andrea K. Townsend"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"05","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Pete Guiden"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Environmental Studies","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"06","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's signature required","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Kwabena Edusei"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 120","title":"Second Term French","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"Increased instruction in aural comprehension, speaking, reading and writing. In 120, students engage in more in-depth conversation topics and writing assignments about everyday life and cultural topics related to French-speaking areas around the world. Four hours of class, with additional independent drill and laboratory work as well as Internet exploration. Prerequisites, 110 or placement in 111/120. Although a natural continuation of 110, 120 can be taken independently. First-year students who follow the sequence to 140 may qualify to attend Hamilton in France.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/100/frnch-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Mireille G. Koukjian"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Mireille G. Koukjian"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 140","title":"Intermediate French II","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"This intermediate-level French course is based on film scripts, films, and other related materials. Focus on speaking, listening, reading, and writing to express ideas and arguments with precision and nuance. Review of advanced grammar structures and expansion of vocabulary to develop strategies for description and narration and in expressing preferences and opinions, Analysis of texts and films develops critical thinking in French. Activities such as debate, interviews, discussion, and written assignments. Conducted entirely in French.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/100/frnch-140","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Paule Carbonnel"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 200W","title":"Written & Oral Argumentatn","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"An intensive course focusing on oral and written argumentation, proper nuanced expression, improvement of syntax and vocabulary-building strategies through the exploration of contemporary literary and cultural texts. Heritage speakers with instructor permission. Regular class meetings plus a weekly discussion session with a teaching assistant.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/200/frnch-200","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Zachary Desjardins-Mooney"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 212","title":"Francophone Crime Fiction","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"Study the genre that has kept readers up at night for 200 years. This course follows French and Francophone thieves, murderers, and detectives from the sensational serial novels of the 1840s to Netflix's Lupin. Readings range from convict-turned-policeman Vidocq, the real-life inspiration for Hugo's Jean Valjean in Les Misérables, to Fred Vargas and Leïla Slimani, two of the women reshaping the genre today. The course also tracks crime off the page and into the headlines, from the tabloid faits divers to today's true-crime podcasts, and asks why the audience can't look away. Topics vary from semester to semester. May be retaken for credit. Taught in French.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/200/frnch-212","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Paule Carbonnel"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 250","title":"Contemporary France","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"Exploration of selected trends and current events in the larger context of France's historical, geopolitical and sociopolitical forces. The course alternates readings from a conventional textbook and various media covering current events. It makes use of text, images and sound. Topics include geopolitics; youth cultures; social, national and religious identities, labor laws, and the environment. There will be room to integrate debates likely to arise during the semester, especially around the presidential elections. Engaged participation in class discussion expected. Short written and oral assignments and a longer multi-media final research paper/presentation. Taught in French.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/200/frnch-250","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Martine Guyot-Bender"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 311","title":"Health Humanities","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"This course serves as an introduction to contemporary French and Francophone health humanities. How have French and francophone authors portrayed health, illness, and medicine? How can medicine and literature inform one another, and in what ways can literary texts challenge the medical gaze and prevailing medical and psychiatric norms? Close readings of novels, poetry, letters, photographs, films, and medical writings. Attention paid to how dominant norms of citizenship, disability, gender, race, and sexuality shape medical science and discourse. Readings include works by Deligny, Duras, Ernaux, Guibert, de Kerangal, Malinconi, Manicom, and de Vigan, alongside theoretical texts by Fanon, Foucault, and Malabou. Taught in French.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/300/frnch-311","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Zachary Desjardins-Mooney"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 428","title":"Post-war Cinematographic","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"Based on three chapters of recent French history (the Occupation and the Holocaust; relationship with Algeria; May 1968 and social unrest), investigates how filmmakers mediate individual and national memories through moving images. The films will be considered in the context of recent historiographical material, theoretical discourse on cinema, and very specific cultural policies in France, as well as popular events around cinema. Includes about 10 movies. Some Friday afternoons will be reserved for film screenings. Taught in French.","cross_listed":["CNMS 428"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/400/frnch-428","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Martine Guyot-Bender"]}]},
{"code":"FRNCH 550","title":"Sr Indep Research Project","department":"French","credits":1,"description":"Independent study program consisting of the preparation and oral presentation of a paper in French. Only students having an average of A- or better in courses counting toward the concentration at the end of the first semester of the senior year may qualify. Registration is only by consent of instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/frnch-french-and-francophone-studies/500/frnch-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Jessica N. Burke"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 112","title":"The Marine Environment","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the physical, chemical and biological nature of the marine environment. Topics include marine geology, seawater composition, atmosphere/climate, ocean circulation, waves, tides, coastal processes, life in the sea, ocean resources and marine pollution. Three hours of class and two hours of laboratory, TBA. Not open to students who have taken any other course in Principles of Geoscience.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/100/geosc-112","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1011","types":["Environmental Studies","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1011","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"R","room":"1011","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Kristopher M. Kusnerik"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 115","title":"Evolution and Environment","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"This course looks at the intersection of life and earth history as a lens for understanding fundamental concepts in geology. Evolution will be studied on multiple scales from the origins of life in deep time, to hominin evolution in the Quaternary, to looking ahead to how we may have to culturally evolve in the face of anthropogenic climate change. Three hours of class and two hours of laboratory. Not open to students who have taken any other course in Principles of Geoscience.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/100/geosc-115","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1035","types":["Environmental Studies","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1035","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"M","room":"1035","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Catherine Beck"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 209","title":"Hydrogeology","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"Hydrogeology is the study of surface water and groundwater, with a focus on groundwater. The course covers how water enters the subsurface, how groundwater interacts with surface water and flows in the subsurface through aquifers, and how it interacts with the surrounding soil and rock, affecting water chemistry. The course prepares students to address many environmental challenges that hydrogeologists study, including sustainable water supply, food and energy production, environmental protection and conservation, groundwater contamination, and climate change. The course prepares students to address such challenges by developing their ability to investigate groundwater systems, perform common laboratory and field methods, and solve complex problems in hydrogeology. The course consists of three hours of class and three hours of laboratory every week, with some outdoor field trips.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/200/geosc-209","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1030","types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":15,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1030","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"R","room":"1030","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Sarah Jonathan"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 220","title":"Earth Materials","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"Minerals and rocks are the fundamental building blocks of planet Earth. This course will cover the physical and chemical properties of minerals and rocks, as well as the fundamental role that these \"Earth Materials\" play in the Earth system and their relevance to society. Three hours of class and three hours of lab with a field trip.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/200/geosc-220","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1033","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":"1033","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"M","room":"1033","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Amy Moser"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 223","title":"The Critical Zone","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the science behind the critical zone, the dynamic zone at earth's surface that extends from the base of weathered bedrock to the top of the vegetation canopy and encompasses the intersection of rock, soil, water, air, and life. Throughout this course, we will investigate the complex processes that drive the movement of materials through our environment and shape ecosystems and human societies. Examples of topics include soils and soil formation, nutrient cycling, and rock-climate interactions.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/200/geosc-223","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"1030","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"1030","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Sarah Jonathan"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 230","title":"Structural Geology","department":"Geosciences","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to concepts of deformation of the lithosphere and the processes and products of rock deformation from microscopic to plate scales. Neotectonics as well as reconstruction of geologic history from the record of deformed rocks. Six hours of class/laboratory with field trip.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/200/geosc-230","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"1016","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":24,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"1016","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"T","room":"1016","type":"LAB","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Nicolas M. Roberts"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 298","title":"Geoscience Research","department":"Geosciences","credits":0.25,"description":"Geoscience research under the supervision of a faculty member. May be repeated for credit. Students may count up to 1 credit of geoscience research toward the concentration. One quarter, one half, or one credit per semester. No senior concentrators.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/200/geosc-298","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Emily A. Baker"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Catherine Beck"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Amy Moser"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Kristopher M. Kusnerik"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 511","title":"Senior Project","department":"Geosciences","credits":0.5,"description":"A two-term course during which concentrators pursue an independent research project and present the results to the department. Proposals for independent research projects must be completed in the spring semester of the student's junior year. Must be taken in conjunction with the Department's two-term, Senior Seminar (GEOSC 551 / 552). Both courses are required for the concentration. One-half course credit each for 510 and 511.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Emily A. Baker"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Catherine Beck"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Nicolas M. Roberts"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Kristopher M. Kusnerik"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"05","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Amy Moser"]}]},
{"code":"GEOSC 552","title":"Senior Seminar II","department":"Geosciences","credits":0.5,"description":"A half-credit seminar to be taken in conjunction with GEOSC 511 (Senior Project). In this course students will continue to examine, discuss, and reflect upon structural and institutional hierarchies in the geosciences based on the social categories of race, class, gender, ethnicity, nationality, religion, sexuality, age, and/or disabilities. Students will also discuss ethical issues related to work and behavior as geoscience professionals. This seminar, taken along with its companion fall semester seminar (GEOSC 551) will satisfy the department's SSIH requirement.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/geosc-geosciences/510500/geosc-552","sections":[{"days":"R","room":"1011","types":["Senior Project","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Taylor Science Center","capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Seniors Only","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":"1011","type":"LEC","building":"Taylor Science Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Kristopher M. Kusnerik"]}]},
{"code":"GERMN 120","title":"Second Term German","department":"German","credits":1,"description":"Continued development of German grammar and its use in aural comprehension, speaking, reading and writing. Readings in literature and culture supplemented with video recordings. Three hours of class, with additional sessions and laboratory work.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/germn-german-studies/100/germn-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:20:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:20","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Lena Truper"]}]},
{"code":"GERMN 140","title":"Intro. German Lit & Culture","department":"German","credits":1,"description":"Continued development of German grammar and vocabulary with cultural and literary texts, including works by Kafka, Drrenmatt and Brecht, and song texts by contemporary Liedermacher. Practice in oral and written work. Prerequisites, 130 or consent of instructor. Taught in German.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/germn-german-studies/100/germn-140","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Franziska Schweiger"]}]},
{"code":"GERMN 284","title":"Northern Renaissance Art","department":"German","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the distinctive ways in which art was crafted and consumed in northern Europe during the age of Renaissance and Reformation. We will examine paintings and prints, propaganda and princely splendor, and carved altarpieces and ceremonial armor against the backdrop of both city and court, while considering issues of religious function, social use, and the economic history of the arts. The course also fosters a critical awareness of the methods of art history by drawing attention to scholarship on Northern art that has, in many ways, laid the foundations for modernity.","cross_listed":["ARTH 284","MDRST 284"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/germn-german-studies/200/germn-284","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"GERMN 310","title":"Survey of German Lit","department":"German","credits":1,"description":"Study of major writers and literary movements from the 18th century to today, including authors from Germany, Austria and the former GDR. Works will include poetry, drama and short prose. Designed as preparation for upper-level literature seminars. Taught in German.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/germn-german-studies/300/germn-310","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Lena Truper"]}]},
{"code":"GERMN 500","title":"Senior Project","department":"German","credits":1,"description":"A senior thesis required of all concentrators in the department. Open to concentrators only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/germn-german-studies/500/germn-500","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Franziska Schweiger"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 112","title":"Comparative Politics","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the study of non-American national political systems, emphasizing authority, legitimacy and processes of state- and nation-building. Comparison of alternate forms of political development in selected Western and non-Western countries. Offered as Proseminar & Writing-Intensive at least once per year. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/100/govt-112","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Sharon W. Rivera"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Sharon W. Rivera"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 114W","title":"International Relations","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the theory and practice of world politics. Emphasis on the changing structure of the international system; the role of the nation-state and non-state actors; patterns of conflict and cooperation; the use of force, diplomacy and ideology; the interplay between politics and economics. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/100/govt-114","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["David W. Rivera"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs and Sophomores Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["David W. Rivera"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Pedram Maghsoud-Nia"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Pedram Maghsoud-Nia"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 116","title":"Amer Political Process","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the study of American national institutions, the public policy-making process and, in general, the distribution of political power in American society. Offered as Writing-Intensive & Proseminar at least once per year. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/100/govt-116","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Annabelle G. Hutchinson"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 117W","title":"Intro Political Theory","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Survey of selected political theorists from Plato to the present. Examination of questions of liberty, equality, justice and community. (Proseminar and writing-intensive in the Fall.) (Political Theory)","cross_listed":["PHIL 117W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/100/govt-117","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]},{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 201W","title":"Intro Feminist Thought","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"An interdisciplinary examination of the history and contemporary practice of feminist thought. Topics include the history of feminist thought in Western culture, the broadening and complication of that canon to include examinations of race, class, gender, sexuality, ableism and ageism, and the implications of global feminist thought. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":["WMGST 201W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-201","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Amy Marvin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 206","title":"Soft Power & the Global South","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"For decades, power in terms of foreign policy strategies was understood through the lens of hard power (e.g., coercive diplomacy & military intervention) to enforce national interests. Soft power, or the ability for states to shape the preferences of others through attractive power, is employed by states to influence how governments and populations in other countries perceive them. From educational exchanges to music and sports, this course examines soft power approaches employed by both large states and smaller ones mainly from the Global South. Through guest speakers, film, and in-class activities, the class will investigate how a country's size and resources impact its soft power strategies and their effectiveness.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-206","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Kira D. Jumet"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 218","title":"Politics of Africa","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Comparative examination of the domestic politics of sub-Saharan Africa. Central focus on explaining the recent rise of both multi-party democracy and state collapse across the continent. Examination of the colonial legacy, the nature of the African state, ethnic conflict, class divisions, the role of the military and the problems of economic underdevelopment. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-218","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lindsey Pruett"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 223","title":"Parties and Ideologies","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This course provides a basic overview of the major questions that have motivated academic research on political parties and ideologies in the United States for the past half-century. These questions include: why are parties formed? Do parties matter? How did conservatism and liberalism develop as distinct ideologies? How did they come to be adopted by the two major parties? And how do these parties' ideologies influence American politics? We will examine these questions through a combination of academic articles and books, news articles, and historical accounts. By the end of this course, students will be conversant with the historical development of - and major scholarly debates over - American political parties and ideologies.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-223","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Foy-Sutherland"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 230","title":"Data Analysis","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Drawing implications from data has become essential to building successful careers. This course guides students through developing a meaningful social science research question and designing an empirical study suitable for investigating their questions. We consider how to conduct transparent, rigorous, and evidence-based research, engage with statistical tools, and learn the basics of R. Not open to students who have taken ECON 266. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-230","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":25,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Catherine Chen"]},{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":25,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Catherine Chen"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 237","title":"Russia and the World","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The course will examine Russia's relations with both its post-Soviet neighbors and the West from the Tsarist era to the present. Topics to be covered include: the formation of the Russian Empire, the Cold War, the evolution of Russian-Western relations since the collapse of communism, Russia's annexation of Crimea in 2014, and the outbreak of a major war with Ukraine in 2022. A central theme of the course will be the evolution of Russian national identity, especially as it relates to Russia's status as an empire and its relationship with the West. (International Relations)","cross_listed":["RSNST 237"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-237","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["David W. Rivera"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 239","title":"Digital Human Rights","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Research based on Open Source Intelligence (OSINT) is a technique pioneered by non-profit organizations and academic institutions to improve the transparency and fact-checking of investigative journalism and strengthen legal cases in the area of international human rights. This course provides students with an overview of these techniques and considers associated ethical, privacy, and technological concerns. Students will use techniques learned in class to gather and analyze OSINT materials related to international human rights violations in the context of the Russo-Ukrainian War. There is a required field trip to Washington, D.C., which involves staying overnight.","cross_listed":["COLEG 239"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-239","sections":[{"days":"M","room":null,"types":["Experiential Learning"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Juniors and Seniors Only","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Sharon W. Rivera"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 240","title":"International Security","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the use of force to achieve political objectives by both state and non-state actors. It explores the origins, conduct, and outcomes of international and civil wars; the use of terrorism and other forms of violence against civilians; the motivations of people that participate in political violence; and the lasting effects of such participation. Includes both historical and contemporary cases, ranging from the First World War to the current conflict in Syria. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-240","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Lindsey Pruett"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 241","title":"Constitutional Law","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Analysis of constitutional doctrines through major cases. Function of the Supreme Court as an instrument of government and arbiter of public policy. Doctrines include judicial review, federalism, interstate commerce, due process and questions of individual rights. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-241","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Co-Lab Course","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Frank M. Anechiarico"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 249","title":"Amer Political Thought","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"What does it mean to be an American? What are the principles of American politics? Considers the answers of Thomas Jefferson, James Madison, Alexander Hamilton, Alexis de Tocqueville, W.E.B. DuBois, Emma Goldman, John Dewey, Leo Strauss and William E. Connolly. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-249","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Robert W. Martin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 251","title":"Intro Public Policy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The study of policy analysis using and comparing a variety of disciplinary and analytic traditions. Consideration of controversies over particular policies at the national and local level and the premises underlying them. Examination of methods and principles used in formulating and evaluating public policy. Open to seniors with consent of instructor. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["PPOL 251"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-251","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Heather A. Sullivan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 273W","title":"Justice Lab Seminar","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This seminar forms part of the justice lab program. It focuses on issues of politics and policy. This course forms part of the Justice Lab. Students must apply to be a part of the lab.","cross_listed":["PPOL 273W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-273","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Spring 2027 Justice Lab students only.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Heather A. Sullivan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 281","title":"Common Ground","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the U.S. Constitution through extended role-playing games in a speaking intensive course. Students immerse themselves in two constitutional moments - the 1787 Constitution Convention and a hypothetical new Convention of the States to propose new amendments to the U.S. Constitution.","cross_listed":["COLEG 281","HIST 281"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-281","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course","Experiential Learning","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Ty Seidule"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 285","title":"Intro Environmental Politics","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"An overview of environmental politics, domestic and global. Topics include the environmental movement and its history and values, anti-environmentalism, environmental policy analysis, the relation between environmental science and politics, the domestic and international environmental policy processes, the North-South debate, globalization, race and environmental justice, and the implications of environmental politics for liberal democracy. Students will explore these topics directly and through selected policy issues, including forest politics, sprawl and climate change. (American Politics)","cross_listed":["ENVST 285"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-285","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Environmental Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Peter F. Cannavo"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 291","title":"Internatnl Pol Economy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the development and evolution of the modern global economy and its political impact. Issues include global trade relations, the monetary system and international debt, the role of multinational corporations, foreign aid, imperialism and dependency, industrial competitiveness and the rise and impact of newly industrializing countries such as South Korea and Taiwan. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/200/govt-291","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Alan W. Cafruny"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 305W","title":"Native Indigenous Pol Thought","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This course investigates various texts in Native and Indigenous political thought. Themes may include indigeneity, colonialism, sovereignty, recognition, refusal, and resistance. Readings will center on the works of non-Western thinkers. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-305","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kenneth A. Leonardo"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 315W","title":"Foreign Policies Middle East","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The Middle East is facing a period of instability and transformation. The aim of this course is to explore the foreign policy decisions of Middle Eastern states. Through class discussion, film, and guest speakers, this course will examine the security challenges facing major players in the region (Iran, Saudi Arabia, Iraq, Turkey, Qatar, Egypt, Israel, Palestine, Jordan, Syria and Lebanon). Particular attention will be given to theories of foreign policy decision-making in nondemocratic states. (International Relations)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-315","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kira D. Jumet"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 316W","title":"Civic Republicanism","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Civic republicanism is a political tradition that emphasizes civic virtue, political participation, non-domination, and the common good. Civic republicanism influenced the American founding and the Abolitionist, labor, antipoverty, Civil Rights, feminist, and environmental movements. This course begins with the history of civic republicanism, including classic political thinkers like Aristotle, Wollstonecraft, Rousseau, Jefferson, Douglass, and Marx, and then explores contemporary republican political thought. Additionally, we look at how civic republicanism can inform responses to contemporary issues like racism, sexism, reproductive rights, homophobia, economic inequality, political corruption, threats to democracy, new technologies, and climate change. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-316","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, & Sophomores","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Peter F. Cannavo"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 321","title":"Federal Internship","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Twelve-week internships in relevant DC office. Interns assume some operational responsibility in the office and gain a perspective on the political and policy process in Washington DC. Does not count toward the concentration. Offered satisfactory/unsatisfactory only. Internship is subject to approval by DC Program Director. Taught in person, unless remote internships can be arranged.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-321","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 323","title":"Intern-observation","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Participants in the program are asked to evaluate their experience in government offices through a series of group discussions and papers focused on particular aspects of the internships. Does not count toward the concentration.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-323","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 325","title":"Sem in Wash Seminar","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"An academic seminar focusing on the public policy process and national issues. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-325","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 327","title":"Sem-wash Indep Resrch","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Preparation and presentation of independent research on a problem related to public policy issues. Use of Washington's unique human and data resources required. (American Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-327","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 341W","title":"U.S. Elections","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"This course provides an overview of research on American campaigns and elections spanning the past half-century. We begin by examining elections in theory - focusing on research on electoral history, the role of political parties, and voter decision-making. The latter section of the course transitions to examining elections in practice - looking at research that analyzes key aspects of contemporary elections such as fundraising, campaign advertising, and voter mobilization.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-341","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Gabriel Foy-Sutherland"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 365W","title":"Free Speech Theory","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"Analysis of competing theories of the liberty of expression in the American context. Focuses primarily on contemporary political and legal disputes over such morally divisive issues as \"hate speech,\" campus speech codes, pornography, media and Internet censorship, and the proper role of free speech in a democracy. Examination of the evolution of American constitutional law concerning freedom of expression. (Political Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-365","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Jurisprudnc Law Justc Std","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Robert W. Martin"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 366W","title":"Chinese Foreign Policy","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"China's ascendance as a global power has been dramatic. In this course, we will analyze the external and domestic determinants of Chinese foreign policy and the political, military, and economic implications of this shift in power for the rest of the world. We will examine what drives China's interactions with other states,investigate competing sources of ideas and policy change, and engage in foreign policy analysis. (Comparative Politics)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/300/govt-366","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Alexsia T. Chan"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 419","title":"Topic: Linowitz Sem","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"The Sol M. Linowitz seminars are taught by governmental and policy practitioners who have occupied senior positions in government or with global non-government organizations (NGOs). They offer policy-oriented seminars for twelve Government and World Politics concentrators, who are enrolled through a competitive selection process. Professors may be senior diplomats or foreign service officers; state or federal elected officials; senior state or federal cabinet officers or administrative officials; or senior officials of foreign governments, international organizations, or important NGOs. Contact the Government Department for course details and registration information. Permission of the department required. May be retaken for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/400/govt-419","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required. Retakeable once, for a total of two instances, both counting toward the major. (International Relations)","end_time":"21:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"21:30","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"18:30"}],"start_time":"18:30:00","instructors":["Erica S. De Bruin","Anthony J. Brindisi"]}]},
{"code":"GOVT 550","title":"Senior Project","department":"Government","credits":1,"description":"A senior project required for concentrators in the department who are not pursuing honors. Prerequisites, one 300-level course in government. Open to concentrators only.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/govt-government/500/govt-550","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]},{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Alexsia T. Chan"]},{"days":"F","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"12:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Lindsey Pruett"]},{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"21:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"21:30","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"18:30"}],"start_time":"18:30:00","instructors":["Robert W. Martin"]},{"days":"R","room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"05","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"21:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"21:30","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"18:30"}],"start_time":"18:30:00","instructors":["Alan W. Cafruny"]}]},
{"code":"GREEK 110","title":"Elementary Ancient Greek","department":"Greek","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the language and culture of Greece and the ancient Mediterranean. Thorough grounding in the grammar, syntax and vocabulary of ancient Greek. Reading and discussion of elementary passages from classical or New Testament Greek that cast light on ancient Mediterranean society and culture. For those with no previous knowledge of Greek. Three class meetings a week, in addition to a drill session.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/greek-greek/100/greek-110","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anne C. Feltovich"]}]},
{"code":"GREEK 390","title":"Topic:","department":"Greek","credits":1,"description":"Reading and discussion of original Greek texts that cast light on the history, society and culture of Greece and the ancient Mediterranean. Authors and topics vary. May be retaken for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/greek-greek/300/greek-390","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Sunil Persad"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 107W","title":"US Borderlands","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course asks how territorial boundaries have evolved into a dispersed, interior regime of surveillance and control over the course of American history. Beginning with colonial-era treaties and other practices of boundary-making in early US history, we will turn to examine U.S.-Mexico borderlands as a region shaped not only by movement, exchange, and cultural intermingling, but also by the evolving power of the state and the contested processes of border-making. Special emphasis will be given to the intertwined histories of Indigenous, Mexican, Black, Chinese, and Euro-American communities and to examining how borders are produced, negotiated, and lived, while developing critical reading and writing skills in history.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-107","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Nahomi Esquivel"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 109W","title":"Early Mod Western Europe","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Survey of transformation of Western Europe from the Renaissance through the French Revolution. Focuses on social, political, economic and intellectual developments; examination of primary sources and secondary studies. Stress on basic skills in the study of history.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-109","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Douglas M. Ambrose"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 111","title":"Intro U.S. Hist 1861-2001","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to U.S. history and an overview of the Civil War and Reconstruction, the emergence of the U.S. as a global power, progressivism and the New Deal, the Cold War, the liberal and radical insurgencies of the 1960s, and the conservative revival of the 1970s to the present.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-111","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Nahomi Esquivel"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 112W","title":"Black Power Movement","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"The course will focus on the political history of the Black Power Movement. By 1966, Black Power advocates charged by Stokely Carmichael and the Black Panthers, began a series of meetings with the help of Black politicians like Adam Clayton Powell. By 1967 and 1968, these same activists began planning, holding, and developing rallies for a new U.S. constitutional convention. This class is a political and intellectual history on the legacy of the Black Power Movement, its organizations, leaders, and ideologies. The course seeks to uncover the intellectual history of Black Power as a movement for democratic practice and multiracial democracy in the United States.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-112","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Caleb Smith"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 115","title":"Ancient Greek Hist & Culture","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the legacy of ancient Greece through the study of history, literature, philosophy, and art. This course will also consider the influence of neighboring cultures on Ancient Greece, including the Egyptians, the Phoenicians, and the Persians, to name a few.","cross_listed":["CLASC 115"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-115","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"HIST 146W","title":"Medieval Christianities","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"A survey of the development of the Christian religion in premodern Eurasia and the Mediterranean, from its elevation to a state cult in the later Roman Empire to about 1450. Special consideration will be given to the growth of the church and processes of conversion, the problem of authority versus heresy and heterodoxy, and the relationship of the Christian churches to Islam and other non-Christian peoples. Stress on basic skills in the study of history.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-146","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Sophomores, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 151W","title":"Global History of Oil","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"How was our current dependence on petroleum created, and how can historical thinking help us imagine a better future? This course combines approaches from environmental and social history to examine the oil industry from the 19th century to today. Students explore social and environmental justice movements, the lives of oil industry workers, and our changing global economy, emphasizing peoples and places that are often marginalized. Contribute to contemporary debates on fossil fuels by analyzing museum exhibits in a final writing project.","cross_listed":["ENVST 151W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-151","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 161W","title":"Sages of the Tao in China","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the two most significant sages in Chinese history and the disciples and schools that traced their origins to them. Close readings of texts attributed to Laozi and Confucius, the conflicting interpretations of their teachings throughout Chinese history, and the proliferation of schools that claimed to transmit their Tao. An eminent historian once said, \"Every era has its own Confucius; it's just that there are many different Confucius[es] in any one era.\" This adage, as we shall see, applies to Laozi as well.","cross_listed":["ASNST 161W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-161","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, and 1st Yrs","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Thomas A. Wilson"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 183","title":"Archive Practice","department":"History","credits":0.5,"description":"Knowledge of best practices and industry standards in the archives field is a valuable life skill with professional and personal applications. Managing the lifecycle of records, determining organization and descriptive schemes, and understanding retrieval systems are tasks undertaken by people in a wide range of capacities. Students will learn these skills through professional instruction and hands-on experiences. They will also be introduced to a viable career field, especially for those interested in working in public history, that combines the arts and sciences. Course is graded S/U.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/100/hist-183","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs. Course is graded S/U.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 215","title":"The American Civil War","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examines the causes, conduct and legacy of the American Civil War, with particular attention to the debate over slavery, the divergent social, economic and cultural development of the southern and northern states, the religious dimension of the war, battle front strategy and home front mobilization, the politics of emancipation, reconstruction, redemption and reconciliation, and the Civil War in popular memory down to the present.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-215","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Maurice H. Isserman"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 222","title":"Modern Russian History","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"An overview of Russian history from the emancipation of the serfs to the collapse of the Soviet Union. Themes of the course include how Russia and the USSR dealt with their sense of \"backwardness\" compared to Europe, the multi-national and multi-religious nature of this vast Eurasian state, and the meanings of \"modernity.\"","cross_listed":["RSNST 222"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-222","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 223","title":"Gender&violence Middle Ages","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course serves as an introduction to the field of Medieval and Renaissance Studies. Drawing on multiple disciplinary perspectives, including those of literature, law, history, and art, we will examine the intersection of ideas about the body, gender, and violence in the European Middle Ages. Readings may include the Bible and early patristic writings; the lives of saints; poems and advice manuals on courtly love; depictions of women in the Crusades; Icelandic sagas; and perspectives on the trial of Joan of Arc.","cross_listed":["COLEG 223","LIT 223","MDRST 223"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-223","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Diversity Course","Medieval & Renaissance St","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik","Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 230","title":"England,ireland 1485-1701","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the shifting boundaries of sovereignty and religion in Britain and Ireland in the period from the founding of the Tudor monarchy to the Glorious Revolution. It simultaneously shows how England developed its power overseas through trade, colonization, and privateering. This course illuminates the disparate, multicultural societies over which the English monarchy extended its rule, demonstrating how tumultuous struggles to consolidate and sustain political authority in the 'Atlantic archipelago' corresponded with the global expansion of British commerce.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-230","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Kevin Grant"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 232","title":"Resistance History Thru Film","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Through films and documentaries about places with contentious histories and ongoing political conflicts, this course will examine the solidarities and fractures that resistance against colonial and foreign control brings about in struggling societies. The course will particularly explore the anticolonial struggles of Palestinians, Algerians, Irish, Kurds, Kashmiris, Tamils, and Indigenous Americans, among others, and in doing so engage with themes of sovereignty and self-determination, nationality and belonging, and history and memory. Such inquiries not only have real-world stakes for peoples' survival and political identities, but also shape in profound ways the imaginations and practices of resistance and solidarity across the globe.","cross_listed":["ASNST 232","CNMS 232","MEIWS 232"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-232","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 254","title":"Recent American Hist","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"A survey of American political, economic, cultural and social life from the start of the Second World War to the present. Topics include the Second World War, the Cold War, McCarthyism, the Civil Rights Movement, the sixties and their aftermath, and the Reagan Revolution and its aftermath.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-254","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Maurice H. Isserman"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 259","title":"Early Republic: 1800-1840","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"An intensive examination of the political, social, cultural, and economic history of the United States from the election of Jefferson to 1840. Focus on Jefferson's presidency, Lewis and Clark expedition, War of 1812, Second Great Awakening, slavery and its expansion, growth of antislavery and abolition, Indian removal, foreign relations, and the Market Revolution.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-259","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Douglas M. Ambrose"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 265","title":"African-american Pol History","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This class is a survey of the role of African Americans in American electoral politics from Reconstruction to the 21st century and seeks to help students understand how Black people have navigated both mainstream and radical political ideologies to claim their citizenship in a multiracial democracy.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-265","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Caleb Smith"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 281","title":"Common Ground","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the U.S. Constitution through extended role-playing games in a speaking intensive course. Students immerse themselves in two constitutional moments - the 1787 Constitution Convention and a hypothetical new Convention of the States to propose new amendments to the U.S. Constitution.","cross_listed":["COLEG 281","GOVT 281"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-281","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course","Experiential Learning","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Ty Seidule"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 285","title":"Modern China","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examination of the social and cultural forces contributing to the decline of imperial institutions beginning in the 19th century, as found in the Taiping Rebellion, cultural interaction with Western missionaries, traders, and military and nationalist revolutions in the 20th century. Readings and class discussions consider the coherence of nationhood in Chinese identity and reexamine the \"Western impact\" as a force in the formation of modernism in China.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-285","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Asian Studies","Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Thomas A. Wilson"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 288","title":"The Corporation","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"The Corporation examines how corporations have evolved as economic, political, and cultural institutions, shaping and reflecting the societies in which they operate. Drawing on historical analysis and ethnographic perspectives, the course traces their development from early chartered companies to the 2008 financial crisis, exploring key transformations in corporate structure, legal personhood, and financialization. We will consider how corporations have been shaped by historical forces such as colonialism, industrialization, and neoliberalism, while also analyzing their cultural logics, organizational practices, and symbolic power. Readings will examine how corporations have reshaped labor, culture, and economic inequality, as well as the ways they have been challenged and reformed. By examining corporate influence across time and space, students will gain a deeper understanding of its role in the modern world.","cross_listed":["ANTHR 288"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-288","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Chao Ren"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 289","title":"Gunpowder Islam","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Fifty years ago, the scholar Marshall Hodgson coined the term \"gunpowder empires\" to describe the introduction of new military technology by early modern Islamic kingdoms across Eurasia, specifically in Turkey, Iran, and India. Using this cultural framework to study the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal Empires, this course will focus on issues of imperial power and expansion, social and religious authority, protest and dissent, language and literature, law and ethics, paintings and architecture, as well as gender and sexuality. We will explore the historiography of these Islamic polities along with reading primary sources including court chronicles, local histories, political treatises, religious texts, and poetry in Turkish, Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Hindavi and other languages.","cross_listed":["ASNST 289","MDRST 289","MEIWS 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 296","title":"Wonder & Certainty","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Are we the only rational beings in the universe? Is such a thing as a soul? Can we predict the future? The truism goes that history is told by the victors, and the same is true for the history of ideas -- so, join us for a journey into the history of ideas that haven't made it into modern textbooks. Featuring materials from Hamilton College's Special Collections, this course focuses on a series of case studies from early modern Europe and the Atlantic world that represent pivot points to teleologies in the history of science and thought. The result is a history of wonderment followed by doubt, illuminating divergent and sometimes abandoned paths once frequented by the wisest among humankind.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-296","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley","Christian D. Goodwillie"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 297","title":"Piety, Power, and Politics","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examination of Christianity in America from the era of European settlement to the end of the 19th century. Topics include encounters with Native American religions, revivalism, sectarianism, slavery and antislavery, religion and politics, theological developments, popular beliefs and practices, and the rise of unbelief.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-297","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Douglas M. Ambrose"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 340W","title":"Topic: Archive & Historians","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course examines how histories of borderlands and margins, emanating from empires and nationstates, turn them into simultaneous objects of desire and control. Through the example of Kashmir - long conceptualized as a famed paradisiacal land, which in the postcolonial era turned into a politically disputed and a heavily militarized zone - we will critically parse orientalist, nationalist, academic, and popular histories about such regions in South Asia and beyond. Building upon comparisons with similarly contested sites such as Palestine, the course will center a people's understanding of history through counter historical narratives, literary works, protest music, and poetry. Students in this course can opt-in for co-requisite archival research for an extra 0.5 course credit (HIST-183 History Workshop: Archival Practice and Theory).","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-340","sections":[{"days":"R","room":null,"types":["Experiential Learning","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 365W","title":"Arabs & Jews Middle East","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Deep study of the Arab-Israeli conflict from the 1880s to the present. We will study the relations between Arab and Jewish communities, the roots of Zionism, Western imperialism, and Israeli and Palestinian social and political evolution.","cross_listed":["JWST 365W","MEIWS 365W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-365","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 367W","title":"Himalayan Mountaineering","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"Examines Himalayan mountaineering over the past 150 years, and its roots in imperial expansion, national rivalries, and cultural and social evolution. Topics include mountaineering in the age of empire, George Leigh-Mallory's death on Everest, American mountaineering in the Himalaya, conquest of the 8,000 meter peaks, the role of Sherpas in mountaineering, and the rise of commercial mountaineering. (Writing-intensive.) There will be an optional 2-week field trip to the Annapurna region of Nepal over spring break, conditions permitting. Generous travel aid is available to make the trip affordable to all students in the course.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-367","sections":[{"days":"W","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Maurice H. Isserman"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 383W","title":"Histories of Photography","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This research seminar examines photographs as historical texts. It addresses changing photographic technologies and the many uses of photography in Britain and the Empire from visual art and scientific research to philanthropy and the categorization of criminals. Particular emphasis will be placed on photography's role in representing and influencing ideas about class, race, and gender.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-383","sections":[{"days":"M","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kevin Grant"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 389","title":"Dubois and Black Intellectual","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This course will center the work of sociologist W.E.B. Du Bois and the intellectual history of Black radical thinkers in the 19th and twentieth century of the United States of America. The class will engage students with Du Bois' seminal work, Black Reconstruction in America: 1860 - 1880. The course is designed to examine the history of Black intellectual thought and activism in the United States from 1865 through 2000. With Du Bois guiding the course, students will be introduced to a wide range of Black radical thinkers through American history. Students will analyze how Du Bois' political transformation throughout several decades reflects similar crises experienced by the nation's Black intellectuals as they tried to recover and rebuild Reconstruction.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/300/hist-389","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Caleb Smith"]}]},
{"code":"HIST 451W","title":"Senior Seminar","department":"History","credits":1,"description":"This is part two of the senior project sequence. Students will research and write an article-length manuscript based on their proposal from HIST 450. Following the completion of thesis drafts, every student will participate in an oral defense of their thesis.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/400/hist-451","sections":[{"days":"F","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Senior HIST Concentrators only.","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Kevin Grant","Mackenzie A. Cooley"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 120","title":"Second Term Spanish","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Second Term Spanish continues to build language skills and intercultural competence for students who have taken one semester of college-level Spanish (HSPST 110) or its equivalent previously. The course reviews basic grammatical concepts and introduces the more advanced ones, interweaving language study with cultural studies of the Spanish-speaking world. The course develops proficiency in speaking, listening, reading, and writing in Spanish with the goal of reaching intermediate level by the end of the semester. Course meets MWF for 50 minutes plus a weekly TA session (50 minutes).","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 150","title":"Fourth Term Spanish","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"An advanced language course designed to strengthen and develop oral communication and writing skills. Conversation and debate play a central role and provide a framework for engaging with cultural topics and contemporary issues in the Spanish-speaking world. The course offers a rigorous review of Spanish grammar and seeks to refine students' written expression through an array of formats including argumentative compositions, descriptive essays, creative narration, newspaper articles, and reviews. Three hours of class weekly, with an additional weekly TA session. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/100/hspst-150","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 200W","title":"Exploring Hispanic Texts","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Introductory study of literature and culture from the Hispanic world (Spain, Latin America, and Hispanic United States), with an emphasis on refining oral and written skills in Spanish. Focus on written and oral argumentation, and textual analysis of various literary genres, including narrative, poetry, essay and drama. Additional cultural material studied includes film, art, music, and contemporary social issues. Advanced grammar in context and vocabulary building. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-200","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Not Open to Those Who Took Hspst-201W Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs.","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Not Open to Those Who Took Hspst-201W Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 210","title":"Spanish Literature","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Intensive study and analysis of cultural concepts and selected literary works of Spain. Introduction to basic critical skills for literary and cultural analysis as applied to texts studied. Emphasis on oral performance, student participation and on original application of critical methodology in writing projects. The course includes a required one-day (Saturday) trip to New York City to see a Spanish performance. Taught in Spanish. Not open to senior concentrators.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-210","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Yelsy Hernandez Zamora"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 217","title":"Intro U.S. Latinx/Lit","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examines cultural production of representative U.S. Latinx writers primarily from the civil rights movement to present. The course explores Latinx writers' engagement with language, political status, race, gender, nationality, and generational markers to reveal both the fluidity and instability of the Latinx imaginary. Readings include autobiographical and biographical works, bildungsroman, memoir, historical novel and vignettes in an exploration of the self and one's relation to collective identity. Taught in English. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":["LIT 217","LTAM 217","WMGST 217"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-217","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 223","title":"Intro Hispanic Cinema","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to film in the Hispanic world, exploring the development of different national traditions within Latin American and Spanish Cinema. At the same time, covers some of the basic tools for interpreting and writing about film. Editing, sound, cinematography and mise-en-scne are some of the key terms and concepts studied in order to understand how viewers and filmmakers create meaning in films. Particular attention to the interrelation of cinema and culture, and the intersection of aesthetics and politics. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-223","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Cinema and Media Studies","Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Taught in Spanish","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Jack D. Martinez Arias"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 334","title":"Transatlantic Surrealism","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Dreams, the unconscious, and a 106,460,000 km² body of water we know as the Atlantic Ocean. Originally founded as a poetic movement in France, Surrealism quickly traveled across borders and creative disciplines, transforming beyond its revolutionary roots of the 1920s and 1930s. Using poetic thought, this course traces Hispanic Surrealism's flux between Europe and Latin America, starting with its premodern predecessors before turning to its avant-garde rupture, and ending with its contemporary creators. Materials studied include poetry, cinema, and visual art. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 434"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 364","title":"Cinema of Iciar Bollain","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of the work of Spanish filmmaker and actress Icíar Bollaín (Madrid, 1967), exploring a wide spectrum of cultural, historical, and sociopolitical themes related to contemporary Spain (1980s-today). From her directorial debut, Hola, ¿estás sola? (1995), these themes will be examined through films such as Te doy mis ojos (2002), También la lluvia (2010), El olivo (2016), and Maixabel (2021). Discussions will include feminism, gender violence, migration, cultural commodification, economic and job insecurity, colonialism, and the effects of the 2008 global financial crisis. Readings of historical and theoretical texts will support the analysis of Bollaín's works in class. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 464"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 381","title":"Andean Indigeneity & Modernit","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"The cultural representations of religion, history, means of transportation, media, photography, etc., provide distinct points of view on how modernity has been perceived in the Andes. This course will introduce students to the recognition of those perspectives by looking closely at novels, short stories, and movies, produced from the 19th century to the present day. This course also will explore the diverse ways in which the intensification of modernity has changed the nature of Andean cultural production itself: its language, style, the narrator's and characters' worldviews, etc. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 481"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Taught in Spanish","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jack D. Martinez Arias"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 434","title":"Transatlantic Surrealism","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Dreams, the unconscious, and a 106,460,000 km² body of water we know as the Atlantic Ocean. Originally founded as a poetic movement in France, Surrealism quickly traveled across borders and creative disciplines, transforming beyond its revolutionary roots of the 1920s and 1930s. Using poetic thought, this course traces Hispanic Surrealism's flux between Europe and Latin America, starting with its premodern predecessors before turning to its avant-garde rupture, and ending with its contemporary creators. Materials studied include poetry, cinema, and visual art. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 334"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["James Mesiti"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 464","title":"Cinema of Iciar Bollain","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Study of the work of Spanish filmmaker and actress Icíar Bollaín (Madrid, 1967), exploring a wide spectrum of cultural, historical, and sociopolitical themes related to contemporary Spain (1980s-today). From her directorial debut, Hola, ¿estás sola? (1995), these themes will be examined through films such as Te doy mis ojos (2002), También la lluvia (2010), El olivo (2016), and Maixabel (2021). Discussions will include feminism, gender violence, migration, cultural commodification, economic and job insecurity, colonialism, and the effects of the 2008 global financial crisis. Readings of historical and theoretical texts will support the analysis of Bollaín's works in class. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 364"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Mario Sanchez Gumiel"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 481","title":"Andean Indigeneity & Modernit","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"The cultural representations of religion, history, means of transportation, media, photography, etc., provide distinct points of view on how modernity has been perceived in the Andes. This course will introduce students to the recognition of those perspectives by looking closely at novels, short stories, and movies, produced from the 19th century to the present day. This course also will explore the diverse ways in which the intensification of modernity has changed the nature of Andean cultural production itself: its language, style, the narrator's and characters' worldviews, etc. Taught in Spanish.","cross_listed":["HSPST 381"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Proseminar"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Taught in Spanish","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Jack D. Martinez Arias"]}]},
{"code":"HSPST 550","title":"Honors Project","department":"Hispanic Studies","credits":1,"description":"Independent study program for students who qualify as candidates for departmental honors. Students will work closely with a thesis advisor(chosen from among the Hispanic studies faculty) who will direct and guide the preparation and oral defense of the thesis. Students will normally also choose a second reader. Students must have an average of at least 90 in the courses counting toward the concentration at the end of the first semester of the senior year in order to qualify. Honors concentrators must normally take the Senior Seminar (400-level course) during the fall of their senior year. Prerequisites, HSPST 400-level course, HSPST concentrator with GPA of 3.5 in HSPST courses upon time of registration.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/500/hspst-550","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Senior Project"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Restricted to Sr. Hspst Majors. Instructor's signature required.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Jessica N. Burke"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 120","title":"Second Term Italian","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"Continued study of speaking, writing, reading and listening comprehension. Highly interactive with a strong emphasis on obtaining both communicative and cultural competency in Italian. Classes supplemented by online vocabulary-building and grammar practice sessions and Language Center projects and activities. This course is only offered in the Spring. Prerequisites, Italian 110 or placement.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/100/italn-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Valeria Dani"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 140","title":"Fourth Term Italian","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"Focus is on expanding vocabulary and strengthening verbal proficiency and writing skills through increased exposure to literary and cultural readings.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/100/italn-140","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 230","title":"Horror, Excess, Obscenity","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"Mainstream representations of Italian culture, art, and lifestyle often suggest that the country revolves around pleasure. Culinary richness, architectural wonders, passionate love, and lyrical masterpieces create an image of Italy that leaves little room for the menacing forces that still haunt it. By fully embracing a dialectical method that values contradictions and brings the repressed to the surface, this course explores what is horrific, excessive, and obscene in Italian culture. Through a theoretically informed inquiry, we will delve into the subsurface of deadly histories, censored literary objects, and extreme filmic experiments to examine not only the conditions that gave birth to them, but also the guises in which their specters continue to influence the present. By staring into the abyss of the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, we will interrogate the politics of seeing what remains after catastrophe and whether aesthetic forms can-or cannot-represent the unthinkable. Myriads of old Gorgons await beneath the surface. Warning: This course will analyze materials that some may find unsettling and/or deeply disturbing. Course taught in English; no previous knowledge of Italian required.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/200/italn-230","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Valeria Dani"]}]},
{"code":"ITALN 301","title":"Stories of Travels & Returns","department":"Italian","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the concept of \"being away from home\" through various representations of travels and returns in Italian literature, cinema, music, and art. In these stories of movements and border crossings, we will reflect on the idea of \"home\" with shifting social and cultural perspectives in Italian culture and society. In their shared themes of home and belonging, exile and displacements, these stories will guide us in understanding Italy and Italian as we know today, while helping us find home in a culture and in a language that is not our own. Taught in Italian.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/italn-italian/300/italn-301","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Cecily Cai"]}]},
{"code":"JAPN 120","title":"Second Term Japanese","department":"Japanese","credits":1,"description":"Continued work in speaking, listening, reading, and writing, with mastery of 99 kanji characters. Four 50-minute classes a week (Monday-Thursday).","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/japn-japanese/100/japn-120","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Masaaki Kamiya"]},{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Masaaki Kamiya"]}]},
{"code":"JAPN 140","title":"Fourth Term Japanese","department":"Japanese","credits":1,"description":"Intermediate Japanese with continued emphasis on oral communication. An additional 108 kanji characters will be introduced. Four 50-minute classes per week.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/japn-japanese/100/japn-140","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:20","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:30"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Haruka Higa"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"75","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Haruka Higa"]}]},
{"code":"JAPN 205","title":"Topic Japanese Linguistics","department":"Japanese","credits":1,"description":"This course explores Japanese phonetics, phonology, morphology, syntax and semantics. Students will compare Japanese with English and examine universal perspectives of language.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/japn-japanese/200/japn-205","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Asian Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Masaaki Kamiya"]}]},
{"code":"JAPN 500","title":"Japanese Seminar","department":"Japanese","credits":1,"description":"JAPN 500 develops the formal, discipline-specific writing skills required for advanced research and thesis work. Students compose analytical essays on topics in film studies, literature, or linguistics, with an emphasis on clarity, structure, and academic style.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/japn-japanese/500/japn-500","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Kyoko Omori"]}]},
{"code":"JLJS 101","title":"Introduction to Law","department":"Jurisprdc, Law and Just Stds","credits":1,"description":"Focus on the American system of law: principally constitutional law, criminal law, and the development of civil rights. Topics include philosophical approaches to justice, English common law, and comparative analysis of French civil law and legal codes in socialist political systems and in majority Muslim countries. Consideration of the challenges to trust in legal institutions from the Supreme Court to local courts and police. Attention also to legal regimes regarding refugees, Native Americans, and defendants with mental illness.","cross_listed":["PPOL 101"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jljs-jurisprudence-law-and-justice-studies/100/jljs-101","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Years","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ralph J. Eannace"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 111W","title":"Ancient Jewish Wisdom","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"An Introduction to the Jewish Tradition and Jewish way of reading.","cross_listed":["RELST 111W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/100/jwst-111","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Benjamin Steiner"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 261","title":"Jewish Feminism","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course examines the foundational texts, debates, and activism of Jewish feminism in the United States from the early 1970s to the present. Students will explore how Jewish women named and theorized gender inequality in Jewish law, ritual, theology, communal life, and cultural practice-and how those critiques reshaped American Judaism. Readings will situate Jewish feminist thought within wider developments in feminist, religious, and American social history. The course also considers LGBTQ Jewish ideas and activism, asking where they converge with, extend, or challenge Jewish feminist concerns. Students will gain a vocabulary for analyzing the movement's central questions, historical evolution, and enduring impact on Jewish life today.","cross_listed":["RELST 261"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/200/jwst-261","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Benjamin Steiner"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 263","title":"American Jewish Literature","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"What makes a work \"Jewish American literature\"? Must it be written by a Jewish author, center Jewish characters or themes, or appear in a Jewish language? This course uses such questions to explore the American Jewish experience from the earliest Jewish settlement to the present, with particular attention to the period after 1865. Through fiction, poetry, memoir, drama, and other genres, students will examine how writers have represented migration, religion, family, race, belonging, assimilation, and cultural difference. Rather than imposing fixed definitions, the course treats \"Jewish,\" \"American,\" and \"literature\" as contested categories whose meanings have changed over time.","cross_listed":["LIT 263","RELST 263"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/200/jwst-263","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Benjamin Steiner"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 271","title":"Isreal Lit Film","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"Analysis through Israeli Literature and Film of the internal and external conflicts that have shaped Israeli society since its founding as an independent state in 1948. The challenge of forging a national identity and identities out of diverse diasporas and cultures. Responses to repetitive trauma in an evolving Israeli identity. Readings and films engage students in moral, political, ideological and social debates and address the many sociopolitical, historical, and geographical conflicts of this small but ethnically, religiously, culturally and politically diverse society.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/200/jwst-271","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Erella Brown-Sofer"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 274","title":"Building Jerusalem","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"Since its foundation, the city of Jerusalem has been claimed materially and spiritually by Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. This course will, through its art and architecture, trace the complex history of Jerusalem from ca. 957 BCE to today's divided city. The art and architecture of the city is contextualized in a way that speaks beyond the often-used and simplistic tripartite manner of constructing identity and history in the city.","cross_listed":["ARTH 274","MEIWS 274"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/200/jwst-274","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"JWST 365W","title":"Arabs & Jews Middle East","department":"Jewish Studies","credits":1,"description":"Deep study of the Arab-Israeli conflict from the 1880s to the present. We will study the relations between Arab and Jewish communities, the roots of Zionism, Western imperialism, and Israeli and Palestinian social and political evolution.","cross_listed":["HIST 365W","MEIWS 365W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/jwst-jewish-studies/300/jwst-365","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"LATIN 120","title":"Elementary Latin II","department":"Latin","credits":1,"description":"Continuation of LATIN-110. Further study of grammar, syntax and vocabulary, accompanied by reading and discussion of passages that cast light on the society and culture of ancient Rome and its empire. For students who have completed LATIN-110 or those who have had some Latin but require review. Three class meetings a week, in addition to a drill session. Language course not in English.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/latin-latin/100/latin-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Jesse D. Weiner"]}]},
{"code":"LATIN 340","title":"The Roman Hero","department":"Latin","credits":1,"description":"Readings, in the original Latin, from Vergil's Aeneid and other Roman epics. Consideration of the nature of heroism and epic poetry, with attention to the history, society and culture of the Roman world.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/latin-latin/300/latin-340","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Cultural Diversity Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Jesse D. Weiner"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 140W","title":"Renaissance Outsiders","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"From artists to utopian dreamers to iconoclastic peasants, the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries are teeming with marginalized people radically reimaging the terms of their own self-identity and rethinking the relationship between selfhood and the larger culture. As an interdisciplinary conversation, this course explores issues in literary, art and cultural history through the study of texts that expose and even help create a changing sense of selfhood in the early modern period. (History or Intermedia)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/100/lit-140","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years only","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Stephen H. Schillinger"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 146W","title":"Black Women's Lit Renaissance","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"The twenty-year period between 1970 and 1990 is often referred to as the \"Black Women's Literary Renaissance\" due to the unprecedented amount of literary and cultural production from black women in the United States. This course explores its profound contributions to black life and literature. Students will examine the intersections and oppositions among political movements, such as the Lesbian Feminist Movement and the Black Power Movement, and identify how these connections shaped the aesthetic landscape of the time. Course materials will include essays, novels, poems, and visual art, featuring Alice Walker, Toni Cade Bambara, bell hooks, Lorna Simpson, and others. (Intermedia or Theory) (SSIH)","cross_listed":["AFRST 146W","WMGST 146W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/100/lit-146","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only. Students are restricted to one 100-level writing-intensive LIT course. 100-level LIT courses may not be taken after 200-level LIT courses.","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 153W","title":"Lit: What Is It Good For?","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"Debates about the value of literature have long been tied to questions about its use. Literature has been praised - and condemned - as a source of pleasure, a medium for the transmission of knowledge, and a vehicle of personal expression. In order to determine why fiction matters, we will examine works that explore the power of literature to shape moral, social, and political realities, including philosophical manifestos, anti-slavery treatises, self-help manuals, and experimental novels. Works by Rousseau, Cugoano, Wollstonecraft, and George Eliot, as well as the film The Servant (1963). (Genre or Theory)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/100/lit-153","sections":[{"days":"MF","room":null,"types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First-Years Only (FYC). Students are restricted to one 100-level writing-intensive LIT course. 100-level LIT courses may not be taken after 200-level LIT courses.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Suzanne Taylor"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 155W","title":"Rewriting Pocahontas","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"Who was Pocahontas, and why are Americans still so obsessed with her? Where does Pocahontas the historical person end, and Pocahontas-the-myth begin? And how have Indigenous writers and scholars rewritten and rethought the Pocahontas narrative? In this course we will trace the historical development of \"Pocahontas\" across genres and media. Texts will include histories (John Smith's Generall Historie), dramas (The Indian Princess, the first American play about American Indians), poems (Lydia Sigourney's epic poem Pocahontas) and films (Disney's Pocahontas and Malik's The New World). We will read these settler-authored texts in conversation with Indigenous-authored accounts of the historical Pocahontas (Powhatan name Matoaka) and the Indian princess trope. (History or Intermedia) (SSIH)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/100/lit-155","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["First Year Courses","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:20:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:20","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Claire Burdick"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 204W","title":"Poetry & Poetics","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course examines how poems work: how they are constructed, and how they produce meaning, pleasure, and cultural value. We will study poetry in terms of prosody, conventions, history, genre, and reception, with the goal of teaching the essential skills of close reading and contextual interpretation. Readings are primarily from the traditions of poetry written in English. Prerequisites, For first-years, one course in literature. No prerequisite for upperclass students. Not open to senior English or Creative Writing concentrators. (Genre or History)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-204","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs. Not open to senior English or Creative Writing concentrators.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Steven G. Yao"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs. Not open to senior English or Creative Writing concentrators.","end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Claire Burdick"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 217","title":"Intro U.S. Latinx/Lit","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"Examines cultural production of representative U.S. Latinx writers primarily from the civil rights movement to present. The course explores Latinx writers' engagement with language, political status, race, gender, nationality, and generational markers to reveal both the fluidity and instability of the Latinx imaginary. Readings include autobiographical and biographical works, bildungsroman, memoir, historical novel and vignettes in an exploration of the self and one's relation to collective identity. Taught in English. Not open to seniors. (Genre or History)","cross_listed":["HSPST 217","LTAM 217","WMGST 217"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-217","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 223","title":"Gender&violence Middle Ages","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course serves as an introduction to the field of Medieval and Renaissance Studies. Drawing on multiple disciplinary perspectives, including those of literature, law, history, and art, we will examine the intersection of ideas about the body, gender, and violence in the European Middle Ages. Readings may include the Bible and early patristic writings; the lives of saints; poems and advice manuals on courtly love; depictions of women in the Crusades; Icelandic sagas; and perspectives on the trial of Joan of Arc. (History)","cross_listed":["COLEG 223","HIST 223","MDRST 223"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-223","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Diversity Course","Medieval & Renaissance St"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik","Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 230","title":"Bollywood Film","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"The course charts a history of Bollywood, India's mainstream Hindi-language film industry. Although dismissed for its use of melodrama and song-dance sequences, Bollywood has long engaged with other global cinemas and critiqued social, cultural, and political trends. Readings and films span romances about gender and nation like Mother India (1957) and Chaudhvin Ka Chand (1960); the 1970s \"angry young man\" oeuvre and its later avatars, crime and terrorist films; diasporic family dramas like Kal Ho Naa Ho (2003); and crossover hits like The Lunch Box (2013) that address the globalized present. (Genre or History)","cross_listed":["CNMS 230"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-230","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Pavitra Sundar"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 232","title":"Toni Morrison","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course is an in-depth study of Toni Morrison's fictional works and critical writing. Students will explore her creative and scholarly contributions to ideas of community and genealogy, as well as to representations of race, gender, and nation. Students will also explore her attention to language, form, and literary theory. In studying both Morrison's formal and theoretical commitments, students will develop an awareness of how literature serves as a vehicle for animating and critiquing social, historical, and ethical concerns in our world. Assignments will include short critical essays, collaborative presentations and projects, and readings will include \"The Source of Self-Regard,\" \"The Bluest Eye,\" \"Song of Solomon,\" and \"Jazz.\" (Genre) (Theory)","cross_listed":["AFRST 232"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-232","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Tara M. Holman"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 238W","title":"Films of the Coen Brothers","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course will analyze the way the Coen Brothers' films play with previously hegemonic myths about the American experience. How do their films negotiate expectations of genre? What happens to meaning when pastiche or appropriation become vital to the narrative and imaginary world of the films? We will watch up to a dozen films, including Fargo, No Country for Old Men, Miller's Crossing, O Brother Where art Thou and The Big Lebowski, not quite in chronological order,. Readings from scholarly and theoretical material, as well as excerpts from many of their sources, from Homer to Hammett. We'll also consider some of the 20th century painting, architecture and style that informs their films. Mandatory out of class viewings. (Intermedia or Theory)","cross_listed":["CNMS 238W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-238","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"},{"days":"W","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Stephen H. Schillinger"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 255","title":"Reconstituting Constitution","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the \"Constitutional Moment\" as a broad sociocultural formation in the decades just before and after the turn of the nineteenth century, and as a recurring American phenomenon thereafter. We will begin by analyzing the founding documents as rhetorical performances, reading the aspirations and anxieties they express in relation to those legible in contemporaneous literary texts. Then we'll turn to political, literary, and cinematic works across subsequent American generations that figuratively re-stage the founding moment even as they radically re-frame its stakes and stakeholders. Likely authors: A. Hamilton, T. Jefferson, W. Irving, H.D. Thoreau, F. Douglass, E.C. Stanton, W. Whitman, A. Lincoln, G. Stein, R. Ellison, G. Lucas, L.M. Miranda. (History)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-255","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Co-Lab Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Spring 2027 Justice Lab students only.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Benjamin Widiss"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 256","title":"American Lit 19th Century","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"A lot happened in this country in the nineteenth century. Slavery was expanded and then eventually abolished. Women fought for equal rights and didn't get them. Indigenous peoples were attacked and displaced - but refused to vanish. Waves of immigrants arrived and dispersed. There was a civil war. Meanwhile, the novel became a mass-market media sensation. Poetry was a set of popular genres that everyone read. Melodramas attracted large crowds. New versions of American philosophy rose and fell. People tried to write their life stories. In this course we will read some of those novels, poems, autobiographies, and philosophical essays (by Stowe, Wheatley, Douglass, Thoreau, and Whitman, among others) against the historical backdrop of war, immigration, forced removal, misogyny, and racism. (Genre or History) (SSIH)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-256","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Claire Burdick"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 262","title":"Siberian Text","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course is an exploration of five centuries of writing in and about Siberia. While we will root ourselves in the socio-historical context, our main goal is to engage meaningfully with the very process of the formation of the Siberian Text. By this, scholars of the region mean the body of works that emerged out of the geographical, political, anthropological, and cultural categorizations of Siberia. The plurality of voices and the diversity of spatial experiences engaged in the process of creating the Siberian Text include travelers, criminals, political prisoners, religious dissenters, shamans, hunters, ethnographers, missionaries, vagabonds, and peasants. Probing the fraught legacy of the current spatial discourse on Siberia, the course will examine a variety of media and genres, including official documents, travelogues, oral tradition(s), works of fiction, autobiographies, pictorial art, and film. In addition to the transnational and transhistorical dimensions, we will engage with theories of space and place, imagined geographies, and ecocriticism.","cross_listed":["AIIS 262","RSNST 262"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-262","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ani Abrahamyan"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 263","title":"American Jewish Literature","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"What makes a work \"Jewish American literature\"? Must it be written by a Jewish author, center Jewish characters or themes, or appear in a Jewish language? This course uses such questions to explore the American Jewish experience from the earliest Jewish settlement to the present, with particular attention to the period after 1865. Through fiction, poetry, memoir, drama, and other genres, students will examine how writers have represented migration, religion, family, race, belonging, assimilation, and cultural difference. Rather than imposing fixed definitions, the course treats \"Jewish,\" \"American,\" and \"literature\" as contested categories whose meanings have changed over time.","cross_listed":["JWST 263","RELST 263"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-263","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Benjamin Steiner"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 289","title":"Video Essays","department":"Literature","credits":0.5,"description":"Videographic criticism is a multimodal form of writing. In using sound and image to comment on audiovisual texts, the video essay blurs the line between object and method of study. This hands-on humanities lab introduces students to a wide range of videographic techniques and formats, and some of the theoretical and philosophical concerns driving recent developments in the field. Students learn to not only craft their own video essays, but display greater reflexivity about their aesthetic choices, interventions and compositional process. No prior editing or other digital experience required.","cross_listed":["CNMS 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/200/lit-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"0.5 credit course; Meets 1/20/27-3/12/27","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-03-12","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Pavitra Sundar"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 301","title":"Avant Garde Cinema","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"A history of alternatives to commercial movies, focusing on surrealist and dadaist film, visual music, psychodrama, direct cinema, the film society movement, personal cinema, the New American Cinema, structuralism, Queer cinema, feminist cinema, minor cinema, recycled cinema and devotional cinema. While conventional entertainment films use the novel, the short story and the stage drama as their primary instigations, experimental and avant-garde films are analogous to music, poetry, painting, sculpture and collage. Not open to first-year students.","cross_listed":["ARTH 301","CNMS 301"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/300/lit-301","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Seniors, Juniors, and Sophomores. First Years by instructor permission.","end_time":"22:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"22:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Scott M. MacDonald"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 344W","title":"'earing Accent (methods)","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"Everyone speaks with an accent. But where some people's speech is deemed \"neutral,\" others are cast as \"accented.\" Racialized and classed perceptions about language and fluency infuse literature as well as the realms of education, housing, employment, and immigration. How might we reframe \"accent\" such that it unsettles assumptions about linguistic identity and cultural worth? To what extent are accents visual, aural, and textual, not just oral? Course texts may include: My Fair Lady; Sorry to Bother You; episodes from The Simpsons and Modern Family; creative non-fiction, fiction, and poetry. (Intermedia) (SSIH) Methods","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/300/lit-344","sections":[{"days":"M","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"M","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Pavitra Sundar"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 347W","title":"Shakespeare and Appropriation","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This class focuses on appropriation as a central condition for considering Shakespeare's drama. Along with plays by Shakespeare we will study how his plays have been appropriated by others. In addition, we will investigate how Shakespeare considered appropriation and adaptation a prime method of literary production by studying contemporary plays, ancient literature and a diversity other sources that Shakespeare likely considered in his own composition process. In so doing we will see the exchanges and vital engagement Shakespeare shows with his own reading. (Genre or History)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/300/lit-347","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Stephen H. Schillinger"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 353","title":"Origins of the Now","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the origin stories of several literary and cultural forms that we now take for granted, including free verse, film, the literary magazine, and stream-of-consciousness narration. To do so, we will look at how early 20th century American and British writers and artists worked to reconceive foundational notions such as class, race, gender, self, sex, and time in response to the collapse of traditional Victorian values. Readings and viewings include works by Chaplin, Eliot, H.D., Griffith, Hughes, Joyce, Larsen, Loy, Pound, Toomer, Woolf and other possibilities. Attention given to the various scholarly methods for studying this rich and diverse literature. (SSIH) (Methods)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/300/lit-353","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["American Studies","Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., and Sophomores","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Steven G. Yao"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 356","title":"Women & the Enlightenment","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"This course explores women's relation to the intellectual and political culture of Enlightenment Britain. We will study how warring ideas about the rights of man shaped conceptions and representations of sexual difference and womanhood over the course of the long eighteenth century. Treating \"woman\" as a historical category will, in turn, help us to better understand the underlying assumptions and consequences of our own thinking about gender. Primary texts by Mary Astell, Mary Wollstonecraft, Austen, and Mme de Stael. Feminist scholarship by Nancy K. Miller, Nancy Armstrong, and Toril Moi. (History or Theory) (SSIH)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/300/lit-356","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Suzanne Taylor"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 439","title":"Race & Nation Middle Ages","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"What did race and nation mean in the Middle Ages? We'll first examine English attempts to establish a unified national identity out of a history of invasion and colonization. We'll consider the role of Jews in England, as well as examples of Celtic resistance to English imperialism. Then we'll turn to writing that explores English identity in an international context, to see how real and imagined contact with other cultures - particularly during the Crusades - influences conceptions of nation, race, and identity. Genres include travel writing, drama, romance, and historical narrative. (History) (SSIH)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/400/lit-439","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 465","title":"Sem Faulkner & the South","department":"Literature","credits":1,"description":"Faulkner's major novels, published between the late 1920s and the early 1940s, trace his most impassioned struggle with the legacies of slavery, the enduring hierarchies of gender and class, and the rising specter of industrialization's destructive force. We'll explore the series of radical formal innovations Faulkner arrived at in order to convey these stories, and to convey the inextricability of one from the next - both in American society and in individual consciousness. Readings: Light in August, Absalom, Absalom!, Go Down, Moses, and more. (Genre or History)","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/400/lit-465","sections":[{"days":"F","room":null,"types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Benjamin Widiss"]}]},
{"code":"LIT 497","title":"Sem: Independent Projects","department":"Literature","credits":0.5,"description":"In this seminar, students will pursue independent research projects culminating in a longer paper (25-35 pages) on a topic of their choosing. Seminar sessions will discuss strategies for organizing and drafting a longer paper, time management, the best ways to use research tools, and different ideas about how to revise; later there will be opportunities to share drafts and address issues that arise.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/lit-literature/400/lit-497","sections":[{"days":"R","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Req.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"R","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Suzanne Taylor"]}]},
{"code":"LTAM 165","title":"Latin American Music","department":"Latin American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Through the lens of musicology and ethnomusicology, this course explores folk/vernacular, popular, and classical music from various Latin American locales and Latino societies in the U.S. Lessons and readings include genres, instruments, and performance practices as well as contextualization with history, politics, and entertainment. Course also interrogates what defines Latin American and Latino musical identities. The course includes in-class, hands-on performances. No ability to read music is required.","cross_listed":["MUSIC 165"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"LTAM 217","title":"Intro U.S. Latinx/Lit","department":"Latin American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Examines cultural production of representative U.S. Latinx writers primarily from the civil rights movement to present. The course explores Latinx writers' engagement with language, political status, race, gender, nationality, and generational markers to reveal both the fluidity and instability of the Latinx imaginary. Readings include autobiographical and biographical works, bildungsroman, memoir, historical novel and vignettes in an exploration of the self and one's relation to collective identity. Taught in English. Not open to seniors.","cross_listed":["HSPST 217","LIT 217","WMGST 217"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hspst-hispanic-studies/200/hspst-217","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Marissa L. Ambio"]}]},
{"code":"LTAM 265","title":"Latin American Music","department":"Latin American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Through the lens of musicology and ethnomusicology, this course explores folk/vernacular, popular, and classical music from various Latin American locales and Latino societies in the U.S. Lessons and readings include genres, instruments, and performance practices as well as contextualization with history, politics, and entertainment. Course also interrogates what defines Latin American and Latino musical identities. The course includes in-class, hands-on performances. No ability to read music is required.","cross_listed":["LTAM 165","MUSIC 165","MUSIC 265"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Sophomores, & 1st Yrs. Open to Music majors/minors only or instructor permission.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"LTAM 296","title":"Wonder & Certainty","department":"Latin American Studies","credits":1,"description":"Are we the only rational beings in the universe? Is such a thing as a soul? Can we predict the future? The truism goes that history is told by the victors, and the same is true for the history of ideas -- so, join us for a journey into the history of ideas that haven't made it into modern textbooks. Featuring materials from Hamilton College's Special Collections, this course focuses on a series of case studies from early modern Europe and the Atlantic world that represent pivot points to teleologies in the history of science and thought. The result is a history of wonderment followed by doubt, illuminating divergent and sometimes abandoned paths once frequented by the wisest among humankind.","cross_listed":["HIST 296","MDRST 296"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/hist-history/200/hist-296","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley","Christian D. Goodwillie"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 113","title":"Calculus I","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to the differential and integral calculus of a single variable. Topics include limits, continuity, derivatives, max-min problems and integrals. This course may not be counted toward the concentration or minor. Four hours of class.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/100/math-113","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Qlit Course","Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["James J. Burton"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 116","title":"Calculus II","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"A continuation of the study begun in MATH 113. Methods of integration, improper integrals, applications of integration to volume and arc length, parametric equations, sequences and series, power series, vectors, and an introduction to 3-dimensional coordinate systems with equations of lines and planes.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/100/math-116","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Andrew Stocker"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Rayan Ibrahim"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 152","title":"Stat Analysis of Data","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the principles and methods of applied statistics. Topics include exploratory data analysis, sampling distributions, confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, regression analysis, analysis of variance and categorical data analysis. Extensive reliance on statistical computer software and authentic data. May not be taken by students who have taken or are taking Econ 166, Econ 265, Psych 201, Neuro 201 or Govt 230.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/100/math-152","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Not open to students who have taken ECON 166, ECON 265 or MATH 252.","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Chinthaka N. Kuruwita"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 216","title":"Multivariable Calculus","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"Introduction to functions of more than one variable, partial derivatives, multiple integrals in two and three dimensions, line and surface integrals, Green's Theorem, curl, divergence, the Divergence Theorem and Stokes' Theorem. Prerequisite 116 or placement by the department. Not open to students who have taken 114. The Department. Completion of 216 with a grade of C- or greater carries credit for both 116 and 216 for those students placed into 216.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/200/math-216","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Jose H. Ceniceros"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Andrew Stocker"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Andrew Stocker"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 224W","title":"Linear Algebra","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to linear algebra: matrices and determinants, vector spaces, linear transformations, linear systems and eigenvalues; mathematical and physical applications. Prerequisites, 116 or 216 or consent of instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/200/math-224","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Jose H. Ceniceros"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Courtney Gibbons"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Andrew Dykstra"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 235","title":"Differential Equations","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"Theory and applications of differential equations, including first-order equations, second-order linear equations, systems of equations, and qualitative and numerical methods.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/200/math-235","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Andrew Dykstra"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 254","title":"Stat Modeling&applicatn","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"A second course in introductory statistics with a focus on applications to real data using the statistical software R. Topics include linear and logistic regression, analysis of variance, categorical data analysis, and selected topics in randomization and machine learning. Emphasis will be placed on interpretation and presentation of results. Depending on the topic chosen by the student for their final project, with the permission of the instructor, may count toward the Social, Structural, and Institutional Hierarchies requirement. Students who have a score of 4 or 5 in AP Statistics may enroll with the instructor's permission. May not be taken by students who have taken or are taking Econ 400.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/200/math-254","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":25,"comments":"Not Open to Those Who Took Econ 400 Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Chinthaka N. Kuruwita"]},{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":25,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Chinthaka N. Kuruwita"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 260","title":"Decisions & Applied Math","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"This course introduces applied mathematics in various social contexts. Its focus is exploring how mathematical tools such as calculus, linear algebra, optimization, and algorithms help shape decision-making in real-world applications. Depending on instructor expertise and student interest, applications may include search algorithms, apportionment methods, resource allocation, political districting, and more. By the end of the course, students will be equipped with mathematical skills to analyze complex systems and think critically about how to deploy mathematics to solve real-world problems.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/200/math-260","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier","Speaking Intensive Course"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Courtney Gibbons"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 314W","title":"Real Analysis","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to analysis. Topics include completeness of the real numbers, cardinality, sequences, series, real-valued functions of a real variable, limits, and continuity. Prerequisites, 116 or 216, and 224.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-314","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Robert Kantrowitz"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 318","title":"Complex Analysis","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the theory of analytic functions of a complex variable: Cauchy-Riemann equations, contour integration, Cauchy-Goursat theorem, Liouville theorem, Taylor and Laurent expansions, Residue theory.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-318","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Robert Kantrowitz"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 322","title":"Graph Theory","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the theory and applications of graph theory. Topics include: trees; connectivity; Eulerian and Hamiltonian graphs; vertex-, edge- and map-colorings; digraphs; tournaments; matching theory; planarity and Ramsey numbers. Prerequisites, Math 224 or CS 123 or CS 230.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-322","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Rayan Ibrahim"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 325W","title":"Modern Algebra","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the three fundamental structures of abstract algebra: groups, rings and fields. Prerequisites, 224.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-325","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Saber Ahmed"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Saber Ahmed"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 351","title":"Prob & Stat Inference","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to set-theoretic probability with applications to mathematical statistics. Topics include probability spaces, discrete and continuous random variables, single and multivariate distributions, and limit theorems, leading into the mathematical theory of estimators, sampling distributions, confidence intervals, and hypothesis testing. Prerequisites, Math 216 and an introductory statistics course which may be one of Math 152/252/253, Econ 166, Pysch/Neuro 201, or Govt 230. Students who have a score of 4 or 5 in AP Statistics may enroll with the instructor's permission.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-351","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["Clark Bowman"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 356","title":"Stat Methods Machine Learning","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"This course covers statistical methods in machine learning such as decision trees, random forests and support vector machines The course will use a project-based approach to give students hands-on experience using these techniques by analyzing large and complex real-world datasets. More importantly, they will learn the statistical principles behind these procedures, such as loss functions, maximum likelihood estimation and bias-variance trade-off as well as why these principles matter in real world settings. Prerequisites, Math 254 and Math 351.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-356","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Clark Bowman"]}]},
{"code":"MATH 360","title":"Group Theory","department":"Mathematics","credits":1,"description":"This course is an introduction to groups, one of the fundamental structures of abstract algebra. The course builds on concepts introduced in Math 325. Group theory is used in many areas of mathematics and other STEM fields. This course will explore the basic structures and properties of groups as well as some basic operations on groups. The theory will be introduced and illustrated through classic examples. Applications of groups will also be discussed.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/math-mathematics-and-statistics/300/math-360","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Saber Ahmed"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 146W","title":"Medieval Christianities","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"A survey of the development of the Christian religion in premodern Eurasia and the Mediterranean, from its elevation to a state cult in the later Roman Empire to about 1450. Special consideration will be given to the growth of the church and processes of conversion, the problem of authority versus heresy and heterodoxy, and the relationship of the Christian churches to Islam and other non-Christian peoples. Stress on basic skills in the study of history.","cross_listed":["HIST 146W","RELST 146W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/100/mdrst-146","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"11:00"}],"start_time":"11:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 223","title":"Gender&violence Middle Ages","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course serves as an introduction to the field of Medieval and Renaissance Studies. Drawing on multiple disciplinary perspectives, including those of literature, law, history, and art, we will examine the intersection of ideas about the body, gender, and violence in the European Middle Ages. Readings may include the Bible and early patristic writings; the lives of saints; poems and advice manuals on courtly love; depictions of women in the Crusades; Icelandic sagas; and perspectives on the trial of Joan of Arc.","cross_listed":["COLEG 223","HIST 223","LIT 223"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-223","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Diversity Course","Medieval & Renaissance St"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":24,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph. & Jrs. Only","end_time":"09:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["John T. Eldevik","Katherine H. Terrell"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 284","title":"Northern Renaissance Art","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"This course explores the distinctive ways in which art was crafted and consumed in northern Europe during the age of Renaissance and Reformation. We will examine paintings and prints, propaganda and princely splendor, and carved altarpieces and ceremonial armor against the backdrop of both city and court, while considering issues of religious function, social use, and the economic history of the arts. The course also fosters a critical awareness of the methods of art history by drawing attention to scholarship on Northern art that has, in many ways, laid the foundations for modernity.","cross_listed":["ARTH 284","GERMN 284"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-284","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Laura Tillery"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 289","title":"Gunpowder Islam","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Fifty years ago, the scholar Marshall Hodgson coined the term \"gunpowder empires\" to describe the introduction of new military technology by early modern Islamic kingdoms across Eurasia, specifically in Turkey, Iran, and India. Using this cultural framework to study the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal Empires, this course will focus on issues of imperial power and expansion, social and religious authority, protest and dissent, language and literature, law and ethics, paintings and architecture, as well as gender and sexuality. We will explore the historiography of these Islamic polities along with reading primary sources including court chronicles, local histories, political treatises, religious texts, and poetry in Turkish, Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Hindavi and other languages.","cross_listed":["ASNST 289","HIST 289","MEIWS 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 296","title":"Wonder & Certainty","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"Are we the only rational beings in the universe? Is such a thing as a soul? Can we predict the future? The truism goes that history is told by the victors, and the same is true for the history of ideas -- so, join us for a journey into the history of ideas that haven't made it into modern textbooks. Featuring materials from Hamilton College's Special Collections, this course focuses on a series of case studies from early modern Europe and the Atlantic world that represent pivot points to teleologies in the history of science and thought. The result is a history of wonderment followed by doubt, illuminating divergent and sometimes abandoned paths once frequented by the wisest among humankind.","cross_listed":["HIST 296","LTAM 296"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/200/mdrst-296","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Mackenzie A. Cooley","Christian D. Goodwillie"]}]},
{"code":"MDRST 346W","title":"Madness, Lovesickness, & Jinn","department":"Med. & Ren. Studies","credits":1,"description":"\"Madness\" evokes images of rambling, potentially violent, individuals on the margins of society. How did historical attitudes to insanity evolve in early modern and modern Eurasia? Were madpeople always ostracized or were they simply objects of sympathy and pity? What did hospitals and other networks of care and healing look like for mentally ill patients? In poetry, the pain of separation of the lover from their beloved produced lovesickness. Finally, demonic possession of the brain by jinns (Islamic spirits) was thought to cause madness. How can we understand the literary, religious and supernatural world of these actors without dismissing it? This course will take a holistic view of insanity while engaging with the rise of asylums or other lunatic institutions.","cross_listed":["ASNST 346W","MEIWS 346W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/mdrst-medieval-and-renaissance-studies/300/mdrst-346","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores. First Years require instructor permission.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
