{"code":"MEIWS 210","title":"Minorities in the Middle East","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"This course explores how minority communities in the Middle East-Druze, ?Alawites, Kurds, Yezidis, Armenians, Maronites, Copts, Assyrians, and others-have been formed and transformed from the late Ottoman era to the present. Beginning with the shift from imperial pluralism to nation-state \"minorities,\" it examines how religion, identity, nationalism, and political power shape communal identity and minority-state relations. Case studies span Lebanon, Syria, Israel, Turkey, Iran, Iraq, and Egypt, addressing genocide, displacement, nationalism, citizenship, security, and diaspora. Drawing on history, anthropology, religious studies, and political science, students examine how communities negotiate identity, power, and recognition in a plural Middle East. This course examines historical and contemporary experiences of minority communities in the Middle East. Some course materials address difficult subjects, including genocide, mass violence, persecution, forced displacement, sexual violence, religious and ethnic discrimination, and war. These topics are approached in their historical and scholarly contexts and are essential to understanding the experiences of the communities studied in the course. (Location)","cross_listed":["ARABC 210","RELST 210"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-210","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Salman Elamir Amir"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 232","title":"Resistance History Thru Film","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Through films and documentaries about places with contentious histories and ongoing political conflicts, this course will examine the solidarities and fractures that resistance against colonial and foreign control brings about in struggling societies. The course will particularly explore the anticolonial struggles of Palestinians, Algerians, Irish, Kurds, Kashmiris, Tamils, and Indigenous Americans, among others, and in doing so engage with themes of sovereignty and self-determination, nationality and belonging, and history and memory. Such inquiries not only have real-world stakes for peoples' survival and political identities, but also shape in profound ways the imaginations and practices of resistance and solidarity across the globe.","cross_listed":["ASNST 232","CNMS 232","HIST 232"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-232","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":20,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yr","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Suvaid Yaseen"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 274","title":"Building Jerusalem","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Since its foundation, the city of Jerusalem has been claimed materially and spiritually by Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. This course will, through its art and architecture, trace the complex history of Jerusalem from ca. 957 BCE to today's divided city. The art and architecture of the city is contextualized in a way that speaks beyond the often-used and simplistic tripartite manner of constructing identity and history in the city.","cross_listed":["ARTH 274","JWST 274"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-274","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 289","title":"Gunpowder Islam","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Fifty years ago, the scholar Marshall Hodgson coined the term \"gunpowder empires\" to describe the introduction of new military technology by early modern Islamic kingdoms across Eurasia, specifically in Turkey, Iran, and India. Using this cultural framework to study the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal Empires, this course will focus on issues of imperial power and expansion, social and religious authority, protest and dissent, language and literature, law and ethics, paintings and architecture, as well as gender and sexuality. We will explore the historiography of these Islamic polities along with reading primary sources including court chronicles, local histories, political treatises, religious texts, and poetry in Turkish, Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Hindavi and other languages.","cross_listed":["ASNST 289","HIST 289","MDRST 289"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/200/meiws-289","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 315","title":"Advanced Arabic","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Advanced Arabic is a course designed for students who have completed four semesters of Arabic. The course will help students move from Intermediate Middle level to Advanced. Students will focus on acquiring more vocabulary and more knowledge of the fundamental grammatical and morphological structures of the language. They will also learn about the literary traditions of the Arab World through readings of abridged literary works. The instructor will select the literary work and prepare all the material used for the class. This course is sponsored by the New York Six Liberal Arts Consortium, from an award by the Andrew W. Mellon Foundation.","cross_listed":["ARABC 315"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/300/meiws-315","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["NY6 Courses","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"12:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"12:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Mireille G. Koukjian"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 345","title":"Postcol Theory & Islamic Art","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"How does the study of art and architectural history interface with colonial, postcolonial, and neocolonial structures of power? This is a pressing question for those who study the visual cultures of the Middle East, a region in which these power structures have had considerable impact historically as well as in the contemporary moment. This class will consider from theoretical and practical vantage points how colonial, postcolonial, and neocolonial systems and institutions have governed or influenced the methods of study, scope, and modes of display of historical art from the Middle East. Topics will include colonial-era collection and museum practices, Orientalism and its critics, post-colonial nationalist art historical discourses, cultural heritage in and during the 'wars on terror', and visual discourses of contemporary Islamophobia. Students may pursue research projects with objects or items from the Wellin Museum and/or the Michael Lang '67 Special Collections and Archives.","cross_listed":["ARTH 345"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/300/meiws-345","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Theodore Van Loan"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 346W","title":"Madness, Lovesickness, & Jinn","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"\"Madness\" evokes images of rambling, potentially violent, individuals on the margins of society. How did historical attitudes to insanity evolve in early modern and modern Eurasia? Were madpeople always ostracized or were they simply objects of sympathy and pity? What did hospitals and other networks of care and healing look like for mentally ill patients? In poetry, the pain of separation of the lover from their beloved produced lovesickness. Finally, demonic possession of the brain by jinns (Islamic spirits) was thought to cause madness. How can we understand the literary, religious and supernatural world of these actors without dismissing it? This course will take a holistic view of insanity while engaging with the rise of asylums or other lunatic institutions.","cross_listed":["ASNST 346W","MDRST 346W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/300/meiws-346","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, and Sophomores. First Years require instructor permission.","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Anurag Advani"]}]},
{"code":"MEIWS 365W","title":"Arabs & Jews Middle East","department":"Middle E & Islamic Wrld Stds","credits":1,"description":"Deep study of the Arab-Israeli conflict from the 1880s to the present. We will study the relations between Arab and Jewish communities, the roots of Zionism, Western imperialism, and Israeli and Palestinian social and political evolution.","cross_listed":["HIST 365W","JWST 365W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/meiws-middle-east-and-islamicate-worlds-studies/300/meiws-365","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Shoshana Keller"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 110","title":"Intro Music Theory","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"An introductory survey of the most important aspects of Western music theory necessary for performing, interpreting, and composing music. Topics range from reading notes and rhythms to analyzing and formulating chord progressions. Study of basic musical structures of the Western classical tonal idiom (\"classical\" music) and contemporary popular music practices that have developed from that. No previous knowledge of music required. Students who can already read music in both treble and bass clefs, and have a basic knowledge of (1) key and meter signatures, (2) major and minor scales, and (3) simple rhythms should instead register for Music 210.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/100/music-110","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"106","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs., Soph., & 1st Yrs","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Charlotte Botha"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 113","title":"Intro to Media:integrated Med","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"This course will serve as an introduction to digital technology in contemporary art making as it relates to interactivity, collaboration, and emerging practices. Emphasis will be placed on hands on experimentation and exploration across disciplines. We will cover installation art, web art, digital and time-based art, and immersive environments. Students will gain an overview of emerging digital practices, collaborative production strategies and correlations to social, aesthetic and theoretical issues. Other topics may include virtuality, performance, cyborgs, and network culture.","cross_listed":["ART 113","DARTS 113"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/100/music-113","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"143","types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":"Open to 1st Yrs, Soph, Jrs, and Srs","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"143","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"124","type":"STU","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Anna Huff"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 117","title":"Roots to Country Music","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"Study of country music from its roots in cowboy songs, fiddle tunes, blues, bluegrass, and gospel hymns to current artists like The [Dixie] Chicks, Taylor Swift, and Brad Paisley. Artists include the Carter Family, Hank Williams, Patsy Cline, Dolly Parton, Willie Nelson, Emmylou Harris, Lynyrd Skynyrd, & Garth Brooks. Study of the musical elements, social class, gender roles, and cultural contexts of styles such as Western Swing, Honky-Tonk, Rockabilly, the Nashville Sound, Southern Rock, and Alt-country. Includes films such as \"Coal Miner's Daughter,\" \"Nashville,\" and \"O Brother, Where Art Thou?\" No previous knowledge of music required. MUSIC-217 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 200-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 117","AMST 217","MUSIC 217"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["American Studies"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, & 1st Yrs","end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 125","title":"Applied Music","department":"Music","credits":0.25,"description":"The study of music through lessons in voice, flute, oboe, clarinet, bassoon, saxophone, horn, trumpet, trombone, euphonium, tuba, piano, organ, harp, percussion, classical guitar, jazz/contemporary guitar, folk guitar, violin, viola, cello, and contrabass. Half-hour tutorial for one-quarter credit. Based on evaluation of Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory. A fee is charged. Prerequisites, consent of instructor. Students may repeat courses for credit in Applied Music. Students are encouraged to advance to Solo Performance for further study when they are ready, with consent of the instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/100/music-125","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"90","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Registration will begin in January. Instructor's Signature Required.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 126","title":"Applied Music","department":"Music","credits":0.5,"description":"The study of music through lessons in voice, flute, oboe, clarinet, bassoon, saxophone, horn, trumpet, trombone, euphonium, tuba, piano, organ, harp, percussion, classical guitar, jazz/contemporary guitar, folk guitar, violin, viola, cello, and contrabass. Hour tutorial for one-half credit. Based on evaluation of Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory. A fee is charged. Prerequisites, consent of instructor. Students may repeat courses for credit in Applied Music. Students are encouraged to advance to Solo Performance for further study when they are ready, with consent of the instructor.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/100/music-126","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"90","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Registration will begin in January. Instructor's Signature Required.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 142","title":"Group Performance","department":"Music","credits":0.25,"description":"The study of music through performance in one or more of the following: Orchestra, Brass Lab, Woodwind Lab, College Choir, Jazz Ensemble, Jazz Improvisation, Percussion Ensemble, and Piano Duets. Prerequisites: consent of instructor. One-quarter course credit each semester. May be repeated. Students may count up to four credits from among 141, 142, 241 and 242 toward graduation.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"HALL","types":["Group Performance"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Wellin Hall","capacity":60,"comments":null,"end_time":"21:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"HALL","type":"LEC","building":"Wellin Hall","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"21:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"19:00"}],"start_time":"19:00:00","instructors":["Charlotte Botha"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Group Performance"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Suzanne S. Beevers"]},{"days":"U","room":"104","types":["Group Performance"],"status":"open","section":"03","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"19:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"U","room":"104","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"19:30","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"18:00"}],"start_time":"18:00:00","instructors":["Heather R. Buchman"]},{"days":"U","room":"106","types":["Group Performance"],"status":"open","section":"04","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"19:30:00","meetings":[{"days":"U","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"19:30","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"18:00"}],"start_time":"18:00:00","instructors":["Stephanie Carbone"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Group Performance"],"status":"open","sec