{"code":"MUSIC 245","title":"Music in Amer Film","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"Examination of music in American film from silent films to the present with emphasis on the golden age of Hollywood. Topics include the development of musical conventions in film, different approaches of film composers (Steiner, Tiomkin, Rzsa, Herrmann, Newman, Bernstein, Williams), the meanings that music brings to film narratives. Includes films such as Casablanca, The Adventures of Robin Hood, To Kill a Mockingbird, Of Mice and Men, A Streetcar Named Desire, Amadeus, The Shawshank Redemption, Oh Brother, Where Art Thou? Special attention to Hitchcock films (Psycho, Vertigo, Rear Window). Prerequisites, two courses, in any combination, in music, film, or literature. Three hours per week for film viewings in addition to class time. Music 345 has an additional independent project. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AMST 345","MUSIC 345"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["Cinema and Media Studies"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Soph, & 1st Years","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"},{"days":"F","room":"125","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 265","title":"Latin American Music","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"Through the lens of musicology and ethnomusicology, this course explores folk/vernacular, popular, and classical music from various Latin American locales and Latino societies in the U.S. Lessons and readings include genres, instruments, and performance practices as well as contextualization with history, politics, and entertainment. Course also interrogates what defines Latin American and Latino musical identities. The course includes in-class, hands-on performances. No ability to read music is required.","cross_listed":["LTAM 165","LTAM 265","MUSIC 165"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"List Center","capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs, Jrs, Sophomores, & 1st Yrs. Open to Music majors/minors only or instructor permission.","end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"106","type":"LEC","building":"List Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 267","title":"Jazz Connections","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"An advanced jazz course that builds on the topics covered in Jazz Foundations (MUSIC 161), and examines the construction of the \"jazz tradition\" from a historiographical perspective. Considers how the development of jazz, and its construction in the context of educational institutions, both reflects and shapes concepts of race and gender in American culture. Includes an examination of the impact of jazz genres including hip hop, R&B, and gospel, and focuses on the international proliferation of jazz. Registration at the 300-level only with instructor's permission.","cross_listed":["AFRST 267","AFRST 367","MUSIC 367"],"catalog_url":null,"sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"108","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Gabriel Condon"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 270","title":"Intro Music, Sound & Tech","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to the essential tools of 21st-century musicians, with an emphasis on creative practice and critical reflection. Students will learn to record, edit, process, and synthesize sound digitally; produce music using MIDI keyboards and software instruments; distribute their work; and critically engage with topics linked to the emergence of digital audio technologies. Listening and reading will include a wide range of works by Halim El-Dabh, Karlheinz Stockhausen, Pauline Oliveros, Pamela Z, and others. Open to all students regardless of musical background; additional resources for relevant music theory will be provided.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/200/music-270","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":"143","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":"143","type":"LEC","building":"Kennedy Center Theatre/Arts","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ryan C. Carter"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 280","title":"Intermediate Aural Skills","department":"Music","credits":0.5,"description":"A continuation of 180. Development of aural understanding through sight-singing and dictation. Tonic and dominant arpeggiation in inversion, diatonic melodies with simple modulation, further work in minor keys, introduction to alto and tenor clefs, chromatic intervals, harmonic progressions, rhythms in mixed meters, modal scales. May be repeated for credit with the consent of instructor. One-half course credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/200/music-280","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required. See professor for specific meeting day/time information.","end_time":"13:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"12:00"},{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"17:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"16:00"}],"start_time":"12:00:00","instructors":["Lydia R. Hamessley"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 281","title":"Interm. Keyboard Skills","department":"Music","credits":0.5,"description":"A continuation of 181. Four-part chord progression reading, alto and tenor clef, melodic transposition, introduction to figured harmony, chord progressions, intermediate sight-reading. One-half course credit. May be repeated for credit.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/200/music-281","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":[],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Instructor's Signature Required. See the MUSIC department for more information.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Stephen H. Best"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 310","title":"Adv Topics Music Theory","department":"Music","credits":1,"description":"Intensive study of music theory topics, analytical techniques, and compositional approaches applied to common musical forms and styles from different traditions, including Western classical music, popular, jazz, film, theater, and selected world music. Selected repertoire will emphasize 20th- and 21st-century practices.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-theory-and-composition/300/music-310","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"201","types":["Quantitative & Symbolic R"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":"Schambach Center","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"201","type":"LEC","building":"Schambach Center","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Alex Badue"]}]},
{"code":"MUSIC 326","title":"Advanced Solo Performance","department":"Music","credits":0.5,"description":"The study of music through lessons in voice, flute, oboe, clarinet, bassoon, saxophone, horn, trumpet, trombone, euphonium, tuba, piano, organ, harp, percussion, classical guitar, jazz/contemporary guitar, folk guitar, violin, viola, cello, and contrabass. Hour tutorial for one-half credit required for and open only to students who are preparing half or full recitals approved by the Music Department. Prerequisites, successful completion of at least two semesters of Solo Performance, 110 or 210, and consent of instructor. Students may only enroll in Advanced Solo Performance upon completion of or co-registration in 110, 210, or 211. May be repeated for credit. A fee is charged.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/music-performance/300/music-326","sections":[{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"56","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Kay S. Paulsen"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"66","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":["Elena V. Nezhdanova"]},{"days":null,"room":null,"types":["Solo Performance"],"status":"open","section":"90","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Registration will begin in January. Instructor's Signature Required.","end_time":null,"meetings":[],"start_time":null,"instructors":[]}]},
