{"code":"PHIL 115W","title":"Intro to Existentialism","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"An introduction to various theories and expressions of 19th and 20th century existential thought. Readings include Kierkegaard, Nietzsche, Camus, Heidegger, Sartre, Wright, and de Beauvoir.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/100/phil-115","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years Only","end_time":"08:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"08:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:00"}],"start_time":"08:00:00","instructors":["Todd Franklin"]},{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Sophomores and Juniors Only","end_time":"13:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"13:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Todd Franklin"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 117W","title":"Intro Political Theory","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"Survey of selected political theorists from Plato to the present. Examination of questions of liberty, equality, justice and community. (Proseminar and writing-intensive in the Fall.) (Political Theory)","cross_listed":["GOVT 117W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/100/phil-117","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]},{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"02","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Ashley E. Gorham"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 120","title":"Phil Perspective Self","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"What is a self? Does each person have one? Does each person have only one? How is the self related to the soul? Is it unchanging or in constant flux? What is the relationship between the self and the body? Examination of personal identity, the self and the soul as these topics are addressed in traditional philosophical texts, literature and the natural and behavioral sciences.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/100/phil-120","sections":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to First Years and Sophomores only","end_time":"10:50:00","meetings":[{"days":"MWF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:50","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:00"}],"start_time":"10:00:00","instructors":["Marianne Janack"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 132W","title":"Evil","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"When we think of evil, we often imagine Hollywood-style movie villains, people with malicious intent and genius plans to destroy the world. When we look through history, we often find that evil is much more complicated. In many cases, it is perpetrated by ordinary people who have unthinkingly adopted abhorrent views and practices accepted by their societies. The goals for this course are to ask whether we have unreflectively accepted any such views and practices, to determine what the appropriate responses to evil are once we've recognized it and to practice making the world a better place. Topics include: the nature of evil, global justice, animal suffering, treatment of the elderly, civil disobedience, and beauty standards.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/100/phil-132","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["First Year Courses","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":"Open to First Years Only (FYC)","end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Dallas Amico-Korby"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 200","title":"Critical Reasoning","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This course will introduce students to both the formal study of logic and argumentation and to a wide range of creative (and ingenious!) arguments from across the history of philosophy. Not open to students who have taken PHIL 100.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-200","sections":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":"Open to Srs., Jrs. & Sophomores and 1st years Not Open to Those Who Took PHIL 100W.","end_time":"09:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"MW","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"09:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"08:30"}],"start_time":"08:30:00","instructors":["Phyllis Pearson"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 202W","title":"Eastern Philosophy","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"What is the self? What is the good life for a human being? What is the relationship between humans and nature? Are all things interconnected? What is the universe fundamentally like? In this course we will explore how these questions have been addressed in Eastern philosophy, and see how life's great mysteries can be explored in a variety of ways by engaging in the numerous philosophical methods found in Eastern thought. Typical focus is on key philosophical texts from China, India, and Japan, and views often discussed include Confucianism, Daoism, Buddhism, and the various metaphysical, epistemological, and ethical aspects of different versions of these theories.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-202","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Douglas O. Edwards"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 203","title":"Hist West Phil (mod)","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A survey of themes in the works of some major philosophers of the 17th and 18th centuries in Europe, taught using team-based learning. Spurred by advances in science and criticisms of Church dogma, philosophers in the modern era attempted to reconcile the scientific revolution with a broad view of human abilities. They constructed systematic views of the world and our place in it in order to answer big questions, including ones about appearance and reality, the relation between minds and bodies, the nature of knowledge, God, laws of nature, and free will.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-203","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"236","type":"LEC","building":"Kirner-Johnson Building","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Russell S. Marcus"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 230","title":"Philosophy of Sport","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"Examining the nature and value of sport. We explore how key philosophical themes such as meaning, identity, morality, freedom, justice, and reality play out in sport. Examples of issues discussed include what sport is, and what makes an activity a sport; the connection between sport and the good life; the nature of rules and their application in sports; the identity of sports teams over time; whether players have a duty to play fair; the ethics of technological enhancement; equality of opportunity and competition; and the relationship between sport and art.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-230","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Douglas O. Edwards"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 234","title":"Our Shared World","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"Most people think animals deserve some moral consideration---it would be wrong to kick your dog for no reason. But what is the nature and weight of this consideration? We will study 3 philosophical topics with the aim of answering this question. (1) What are animal minds like? Can they grieve, do they have a sense of justice, do shrimp feel pain? (2) How do we resolve inconsistencies in the ways we view animals? We eat pigs and cows, but not dogs and horses. We treat lab rats according to stringent ethical guidelines, but painfully poison them when they get in our homes. And (3) how should we relate to wild animals? Should we conserve species? Should we bring back mammoths and saber-toothed cats? Should we prevent the suffering of wild animals?","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-234","sections":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"types":["Soc, Struct & Inst Hier"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":40,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"WF","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Dallas Amico-Korby"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 237W","title":"Aesthetics","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"This course will introduce students to debates that are central to aesthetics and the philosophy of art. We will consider issues such as what makes something a work of art, why we should engage with works of art, whether beauty really is in the eye of the beholder, and what to do about works of art by morally problematic artists.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-237","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"10:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"10:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"09:00"}],"start_time":"09:00:00","instructors":["Phyllis Pearson"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 242W","title":"The Black Self","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A philosophical exploration of a variety of historical and contemporary works that illuminate and influence the phenomenological experience of being Black.","cross_listed":["AFRST 242W"],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/200/phil-242","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":["Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":18,"comments":null,"end_time":"15:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"15:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"14:30"}],"start_time":"14:30:00","instructors":["Todd Franklin"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 314W","title":"Literary Philosophers","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"What does writing style have to do with philosophical content? We will look at the \"old quarrel\" between the philosophers and the poets, and look at the ways in which philosophy was and is written, to think about what counts as philosophy and why and how we mark it off from its others. Readings by Borges, Calvino, Kafka, Coetzee, Plato and others.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/300/phil-314","sections":[{"days":"T","room":null,"types":["Proseminar","Writing Intensive Courses"],"status":"open","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":16,"comments":"Open to Juniors and Seniors Only","end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"T","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Marianne Janack"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 414","title":"Knowledge and Its Limits","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"An advanced study of analytic epistemology. We will explore contemporary debates about the nature of knowledge, evidence, justification, the structure of the mind, and the significance of truth.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-414","sections":[{"days":"W","room":"109","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"W","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Phyllis Pearson"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 416","title":"Wittgenstein","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"A broad study of themes through Wittgenstein's work, including the picture-theory, naming, rule-following, meaning, skepticism, and truth. While our focus will be on Wittgenstein's work, we will also spend time on his intellectual forebears and those he influenced, including Frege, Russell, Anscombe, Quine, Kripke, and Diamond.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-416","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"14:15:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"14:15","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Russell S. Marcus"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 430","title":"Plato","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"An intensive study of the philosophical dialogues of Plato, exploring interpretive matters, historical settings, philosophical arguments, and dramatic features. Special attention will be given to the primary texts, and some time and attention will be spent discussing secondary literature. The course will span every major area of philosophy (including metaphysics, ethics, epistemology, aesthetics, and logic) just as the works of Plato do.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-430","sections":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":null,"capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"11:45:00","meetings":[{"days":"TR","room":null,"type":"LEC","building":null,"end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"11:45","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"10:30"}],"start_time":"10:30:00","instructors":["Justin C. Clark"]}]},
{"code":"PHIL 443","title":"Detachment","department":"Philosophy","credits":1,"description":"How much should we care about what happens in our lives? If we care too much, we risk being mired in anxiety and pain, but if we care too little, we seem to lose something central to our humanity. In this seminar we will explore how various philosophical traditions and ages, from East to West, from Ancient to Contemporary, have aimed to resolve this issue through the notion of detachment. We will consider how detachment-in its various forms-can form a central part of a good human life. We also explore critiques of detachment by examining its connections to authenticity and alienation. Examples of traditions to be explored include Stoicism, Epicureanism, Daoism, Buddhism, Existentialism, and Absurdism.","cross_listed":[],"catalog_url":"https://hamilton.smartcatalogiq.com/en/current/college-catalogue/courses/phil-philosophy/400/phil-443","sections":[{"days":"F","room":"109","types":[],"status":"waitlisted","section":"01","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","capacity":12,"comments":null,"end_time":"16:00:00","meetings":[{"days":"F","room":"109","type":"LEC","building":"202 College Hill, Phil Bldg","end_date":"2027-05-11","end_time":"16:00","start_date":"2027-01-20","start_time":"13:00"}],"start_time":"13:00:00","instructors":["Douglas O. Edwards"]}]},
